1
00:00:02,037 --> 00:00:05,107
♪ In the not
too distant future ♪

2
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:07,975
♪ Way down in Deep 13 ♪

3
00:00:08,008 --> 00:00:11,112
♪ Doctor Forrester
and TV's Frank ♪

4
00:00:11,145 --> 00:00:14,148
♪ Were hatching
an evil scheme ♪

5
00:00:14,182 --> 00:00:17,352
♪ They hired a temp
by the name of Mike ♪

6
00:00:17,418 --> 00:00:20,422
♪ Just a regular Joe
they didn't like ♪

7
00:00:20,455 --> 00:00:23,392
♪ Their experiment needed
a good test case ♪

8
00:00:23,425 --> 00:00:25,060
♪ So they conked him
on the noggin' ♪

9
00:00:25,093 --> 00:00:27,195
♪ And they shot him into space ♪

10
00:00:27,228 --> 00:00:29,797
[Mike] Get me down!

11
00:00:29,830 --> 00:00:32,967
♪ We'll send him
cheesy movies ♪

12
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,103
-♪ The worst we can find ♪
-♪ la la la ♪

13
00:00:36,137 --> 00:00:39,073
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all ♪

14
00:00:39,107 --> 00:00:42,443
-♪ and we'll monitor his mind ♪
-♪ la la la ♪

15
00:00:42,477 --> 00:00:45,280
♪ Now keep in mind
Mike can't control ♪

16
00:00:45,313 --> 00:00:47,748
♪ where the movies
begin or end ♪

17
00:00:47,815 --> 00:00:48,683
♪ la la la ♪

18
00:00:48,717 --> 00:00:51,585
♪ He'll try to keep his sanity ♪

19
00:00:51,618 --> 00:00:54,822
♪ with the help
of his robot friends ♪

20
00:00:56,690 --> 00:00:58,225
♪ Robot roll call ♪

21
00:00:58,258 --> 00:00:59,827
-♪ Cambot ♪
- Cover yourself!

22
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:01,028
-♪ Gypsy ♪
- I'm not ready!

23
00:01:01,062 --> 00:01:02,830
-♪ Tom Servo ♪
- Hello there!

24
00:01:02,863 --> 00:01:04,132
-♪ Crow ♪
- That's one "o"!

25
00:01:04,165 --> 00:01:07,335
♪ If you're wondering
how he eats and breathes ♪

26
00:01:07,368 --> 00:01:10,172
-♪ and other science facts ♪
-♪ la la la ♪

27
00:01:10,205 --> 00:01:13,340
♪ Just repeat to yourself
it's just a show ♪

28
00:01:13,373 --> 00:01:16,477
♪ I should really just relax ♪

29
00:01:16,510 --> 00:01:22,050
♪ For
Mystery Science Theater 3000 ♪

30
00:01:24,252 --> 00:01:25,987
[doors closing]

31
00:01:35,797 --> 00:01:37,598
Hi there! Welcome to
the Satellite of Love .

32
00:01:37,665 --> 00:01:39,333
Hey, this here
is my science project.

33
00:01:39,367 --> 00:01:41,102
Servo! Let me out!

34
00:01:41,135 --> 00:01:43,972
I-- I think I'm having
some sort of reaction to Ethel.

35
00:01:44,038 --> 00:01:45,740
That's ether, Crow.

36
00:01:45,773 --> 00:01:48,877
Yes, this here is the crown
jewel of my insect collection.

37
00:01:48,910 --> 00:01:53,047
-Hodusrobotus horriblus.
-Servo. Servo, I mean it!

38
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:55,450
Note the strong
and deadly mandibles.

39
00:01:55,483 --> 00:01:56,483
Hey, watch this.

40
00:01:56,517 --> 00:01:58,352
-[Tom makes noises]
-[Crow screams]

41
00:01:58,352 --> 00:01:59,453
[Tom laughs]

42
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:00,588
Stay tuned,

43
00:02:00,622 --> 00:02:02,957
after a commercial break,
we'll tear his wings off

44
00:02:02,990 --> 00:02:04,625
and burn him
with a magnifying glass.

45
00:02:04,692 --> 00:02:05,493
[Tom makes noises]

46
00:02:05,527 --> 00:02:06,828
I'll get the pliers.

47
00:02:06,895 --> 00:02:10,465
Attica! Attica! Attica!
Attica!

48
00:02:10,498 --> 00:02:15,237
[theme music playing]

49
00:02:16,237 --> 00:02:17,705
[Tom] I chase the...

50
00:02:17,738 --> 00:02:19,207
-How do I look?
-[Tom] Oh, great!

51
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:21,208
-Terrific as a matter of fact.
-You're not just saying that,

52
00:02:21,242 --> 00:02:23,343
are ya? Cause I-- I-- I--
I feel kinda bloated.

53
00:02:23,410 --> 00:02:25,380
That's just the formaldehyde,
trust me,

54
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:28,717
-you're a fine specimen.
-Servo. My God.

55
00:02:28,750 --> 00:02:31,352
-I don't believe this.
-Well, it's a...

56
00:02:31,385 --> 00:02:32,820
A black-banded Holomolina.

57
00:02:32,853 --> 00:02:34,722
Do you have any idea
how rare those are?

58
00:02:34,755 --> 00:02:36,090
[Tom] Oh yeah.
They're kinda rare--

59
00:02:36,123 --> 00:02:38,893
Uh, I don't mean
to interrupt but uh,

60
00:02:38,927 --> 00:02:41,295
I believe the Mads are calling.

61
00:02:41,328 --> 00:02:42,997
-Oh, yeah.
-Sure. Whatever.

62
00:02:43,063 --> 00:02:44,965
Really though, I'm impressed.
I mean, where in this kind of

63
00:02:45,032 --> 00:02:46,367
an area did you find one
of these.

64
00:02:46,433 --> 00:02:49,137
Well, our bed and breakfast
seems to be taking off.

65
00:02:49,170 --> 00:02:52,373
As you can see, the bed
is completely filled.

66
00:02:52,407 --> 00:02:54,175
Ted. Barb.

67
00:02:54,208 --> 00:02:55,810
Rise and shine!

68
00:02:55,843 --> 00:02:59,213
Time to get up now. C'mon.
Get up. Get up. C'mon.

69
00:02:59,247 --> 00:03:00,415
C'mon. C'mon.

70
00:03:00,448 --> 00:03:02,217
-[slap]
-[shouting]

71
00:03:03,183 --> 00:03:05,085
Well, well, well.

72
00:03:05,118 --> 00:03:06,587
Good morning and welcome to

73
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:09,123
Auntie McFrank's
Tangleberry Inn.

74
00:03:09,157 --> 00:03:11,725
Auntie McFrank, you gave me
quite a bit of start there.

75
00:03:11,758 --> 00:03:14,162
Oh, what a nice,
cheery wake-up call.

76
00:03:14,195 --> 00:03:16,363
I trust you'll be joining us
for breakfast.

77
00:03:16,397 --> 00:03:19,233
-Oh, mais oui .
-Yes, according to the brochure,

78
00:03:19,267 --> 00:03:21,668
the Tangleberry breakfasts
are to die for.

79
00:03:21,702 --> 00:03:23,070
Well put.
[chuckles]

80
00:03:23,103 --> 00:03:25,072
Auntie McFrank, why don't
you fetch one

81
00:03:25,105 --> 00:03:27,342
of your scrumptious breakfasts
for Barb and Ted?

82
00:03:27,375 --> 00:03:28,810
Oh, good.

83
00:03:29,377 --> 00:03:30,978
Now, what are we having?

84
00:03:31,012 --> 00:03:32,780
Oh, don't ask honey,
I wanna be surprised.

85
00:03:32,813 --> 00:03:34,315
Right, sweets.
[chuckling]

86
00:03:35,182 --> 00:03:36,350
-Now, Clay,
-Huh?

87
00:03:36,383 --> 00:03:38,418
you and Auntie McFrank,
do you umm,

88
00:03:38,485 --> 00:03:40,020
are you...

89
00:03:40,388 --> 00:03:41,588
Partners?

90
00:03:42,923 --> 00:03:44,292
Yes, we're...

91
00:03:44,958 --> 00:03:46,995
-Ah! Here it is.
-Ah!

92
00:03:49,530 --> 00:03:54,268
Uh, now, um, what exactly do
we have...

93
00:03:54,302 --> 00:03:56,972
-Lo Mein.
-Oh, Lo Mein.

94
00:03:57,005 --> 00:03:58,973
I'll heat it up for you,
if you like.

95
00:03:59,007 --> 00:04:00,275
Some like it warm,

96
00:04:00,342 --> 00:04:01,977
-you know.
-No, no. Please don't bother.

97
00:04:02,010 --> 00:04:04,312
Yeah. You know what, I tell you,
we're actually more interested

98
00:04:04,345 --> 00:04:07,515
in maybe taking a look around,
getting to know the area.

99
00:04:07,548 --> 00:04:09,917
Uh, soaking in a little
of the local color.

100
00:04:09,950 --> 00:04:11,185
Ah, the local color.

101
00:04:11,252 --> 00:04:12,720
Yes. We understand
that you have a llama.

102
00:04:12,753 --> 00:04:14,155
Ah, the llama.

103
00:04:14,222 --> 00:04:15,423
Yeah. We'd love
to pet the llama.

104
00:04:15,457 --> 00:04:17,892
Yes! Yes! Yes!

105
00:04:17,925 --> 00:04:20,528
Mike, did you guys put
on the stuff I sent up to you?

106
00:04:20,595 --> 00:04:22,763
It's time for the local color.

107
00:04:22,797 --> 00:04:25,300
Suppose you folks want to touch
the llama, huh?

108
00:04:25,367 --> 00:04:28,368
That's right, huh?
You wanna touch this old llama?

109
00:04:28,402 --> 00:04:30,070
Oh, you just go right ahead.

110
00:04:30,137 --> 00:04:32,207
You can touch the ol' llama
all you want.

111
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:33,908
You can touch
the ol' llama all night

112
00:04:33,942 --> 00:04:35,943
till hell freezes over.
[chuckles]

113
00:04:35,977 --> 00:04:38,680
Not me, though. Uh- huh.
No sirree, Bob.

114
00:04:38,713 --> 00:04:40,247
I wouldn't touch
that old thing.

115
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:41,648
llama hasn't been feeling
so good.

116
00:04:41,682 --> 00:04:44,052
Whew. Smells funny too.
Whoa.

117
00:04:44,085 --> 00:04:45,920
Smells really
kind of rank, you know?

118
00:04:45,953 --> 00:04:47,722
But you can touch it
if you want.

119
00:04:47,755 --> 00:04:50,492
Ain't no law says you can't.
Ain't that right, Mikey?

120
00:04:50,525 --> 00:04:52,527
Ain't there no laws that says
folks can't touch

121
00:04:52,593 --> 00:04:55,195
the smelly ol' llama
if they want, huh, Mikey, huh?

122
00:04:55,228 --> 00:04:55,997
Huh?

123
00:04:56,030 --> 00:04:58,265
You people bring matches
for Mikey?

124
00:04:58,298 --> 00:04:59,600
You shut up, Mikey,
you shut up!

125
00:04:59,633 --> 00:05:01,968
You folks don't give no matches
to Mikey, you hear me?

126
00:05:02,003 --> 00:05:04,538
You hear me? I don't care
what Mikey say or Mikey do.

127
00:05:04,572 --> 00:05:06,307
Don't give him no matches!
I don't care

128
00:05:06,340 --> 00:05:08,208
if Mikey come crawling
in through your window.

129
00:05:08,275 --> 00:05:09,843
Start making a big old mess!

130
00:05:09,877 --> 00:05:11,245
Don't you give no matches
to Mikey!

131
00:05:11,278 --> 00:05:12,347
Don't you give
no matches--

132
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:13,347
-Shut up, Mikey!
-[Crow squawking]

133
00:05:13,380 --> 00:05:14,515
-Shut up, llama!
-No!

134
00:05:14,548 --> 00:05:15,683
-Just shut up, under--
-No!

135
00:05:15,717 --> 00:05:17,452
Don't give him matches!
No matches to Mikey!

136
00:05:17,485 --> 00:05:18,252
Shut up, Mikey!

137
00:05:19,487 --> 00:05:21,222
[Auntie McFrank]
Ted! Barb!

138
00:05:21,255 --> 00:05:23,390
Aren't you going to sign
the guest book?

139
00:05:23,423 --> 00:05:25,427
Please, I want you
to write something cute

140
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:27,495
and nice and enchanting,
please I have to--

141
00:05:30,030 --> 00:05:32,300
You'll pay for this, Nelson.

142
00:05:32,333 --> 00:05:34,702
I was going to let
you watch Holiday Inn .

143
00:05:34,735 --> 00:05:38,372
But, not anymore. No! No!
Instead, you'll visit

144
00:05:38,405 --> 00:05:42,510
with the very inhospitable
Amazing Transparent Man .

145
00:05:42,543 --> 00:05:44,712
Put that in your llama
and smell it.

146
00:05:45,847 --> 00:05:48,917
You give me this pillow,
it doesn't need fluffing!

147
00:05:53,587 --> 00:05:54,422
[groan]

148
00:05:54,488 --> 00:05:55,990
[laughing]

149
00:05:56,023 --> 00:05:57,625
[Crow] You can stop
that now, Mikey.

150
00:05:57,658 --> 00:05:59,827
Oh, we got movie sign!

151
00:05:59,860 --> 00:06:02,197
[doors opening]

152
00:06:13,473 --> 00:06:15,443
[laughing]

153
00:06:17,645 --> 00:06:19,447
[Crow] Union Pacific makes
the best movies.

154
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:22,417
[Tom] Shh, you guys.
This is my story. My story's on.

155
00:06:24,485 --> 00:06:26,487
[Mike] Is that a lung?

156
00:06:26,553 --> 00:06:28,522
[music playing]

157
00:06:29,323 --> 00:06:30,525
[Tom] Ah, the best cast

158
00:06:30,558 --> 00:06:32,593
that Union Pacific
has to offer.

159
00:06:32,627 --> 00:06:33,795
[Crow]
If there's a reverend in it,

160
00:06:33,828 --> 00:06:35,530
-you know it's gotta be good.
-[Tom] Amen.

161
00:06:38,332 --> 00:06:40,602
[Mike] Quite a coup getting
Francois Poulenc

162
00:06:40,668 --> 00:06:41,535
to do the music.

163
00:06:43,872 --> 00:06:47,073
[Tom] Herman Boxer and Joseph
Ansen were the Babaloo Mandel

164
00:06:47,108 --> 00:06:48,575
and Lowell Ganz of their time.

165
00:06:49,477 --> 00:06:53,213
[Crow] Whoo,
Whiter Shade of Pale .

166
00:06:53,247 --> 00:06:56,150
[Tom] Go in peace to love
and serve Union Pacific.

167
00:06:57,618 --> 00:07:00,020
[Mike] So, we have 70 days
in each year?

168
00:07:00,053 --> 00:07:01,555
-[Tom] Pretty much.
-[Crow] We're gonna have to go

169
00:07:01,588 --> 00:07:03,590
a long way
to get back to train safety.

170
00:07:05,593 --> 00:07:07,595
[Tom] Our car is kind of
a downer tonight.

171
00:07:08,762 --> 00:07:10,330
[Mike] It's up
with brakemen.

172
00:07:12,667 --> 00:07:14,435
[Crow] Castrati's greatest hits.

173
00:07:15,537 --> 00:07:18,105
[Tom] What, let's visit
Sister Bertrille.

174
00:07:18,138 --> 00:07:20,273
[reverend] It is written
in the Old Testament,

175
00:07:20,307 --> 00:07:23,077
to each of us,
this allotment of years,

176
00:07:23,110 --> 00:07:24,645
three score and ten.

177
00:07:24,678 --> 00:07:25,847
[Crow] I sure tore
through mine.

178
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:28,782
[reverend] And in these years,
there is the time of man.

179
00:07:28,817 --> 00:07:30,552
[Tom] Yeah, shame
he died, huh?

180
00:07:30,585 --> 00:07:33,220
[reverend] Time to fulfill
his destiny.

181
00:07:33,253 --> 00:07:35,222
To work together
with his fellow man

182
00:07:35,288 --> 00:07:36,957
in the wonderfully,
complex pattern

183
00:07:36,990 --> 00:07:38,225
of everyday life.

184
00:07:38,292 --> 00:07:40,528
[Crow] Ah, they really captured
the complexity.

185
00:07:40,595 --> 00:07:42,062
[reverend] A time to be born,

186
00:07:42,095 --> 00:07:43,730
-[Tom] Oh yum.
-[reverend] A time to grow.

187
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:47,602
[reverend] To become aware
of the loveliness

188
00:07:47,635 --> 00:07:49,103
of all things living.

189
00:07:49,137 --> 00:07:50,905
[Tom] Sure, this beats
having friends.

190
00:07:50,972 --> 00:07:53,573
[reverend] In the call
of youth to youth,

191
00:07:53,607 --> 00:07:55,577
to learn the happiest lesson
of all...

192
00:07:55,610 --> 00:07:56,810
[Crow] Dishes.

193
00:07:56,843 --> 00:07:59,213
...that the fullness of life
can only be tasted

194
00:07:59,247 --> 00:08:01,315
when our happiness
is shared with others.

195
00:08:01,348 --> 00:08:02,148
[Tom laughing]

196
00:08:02,183 --> 00:08:03,350
[Tom] Alright. Go dad!

197
00:08:03,383 --> 00:08:05,553
[reverend] Later, a time
of maturity,

198
00:08:05,587 --> 00:08:08,522
-to reflect, to relax,
-[Tom] It all sucked.

199
00:08:08,555 --> 00:08:10,892
-[reverend] ...to enjoy.
-[Crow] I regret everything.

200
00:08:10,925 --> 00:08:14,828
[reverend] Yes, this is the
heritage to which we are born.

201
00:08:14,862 --> 00:08:17,632
The blessings to which we may
all look forward.

202
00:08:17,665 --> 00:08:18,832
[Tom] Really going through
the funerals.

203
00:08:18,867 --> 00:08:21,102
[reverend] Yet,
here in my parish,

204
00:08:21,168 --> 00:08:23,637
there are some
who can only look back.

205
00:08:23,670 --> 00:08:24,672
[Crow] I hate them.

206
00:08:24,705 --> 00:08:26,607
[reverend] They are the victims,

207
00:08:26,640 --> 00:08:28,675
unable to reap
the full enjoyment

208
00:08:28,742 --> 00:08:30,377
of the days of their years.

209
00:08:30,410 --> 00:08:31,912
[Mike] Chief Justice,
Earl Warren.

210
00:08:31,945 --> 00:08:34,315
[reverend] They are the victims
of themselves.

211
00:08:34,348 --> 00:08:35,717
[Tom] I hate themselves.

212
00:08:37,585 --> 00:08:39,487
[reverend] My church
is in a railroad town,

213
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:42,122
not far from a big freight
terminal and repair shop.

214
00:08:42,155 --> 00:08:44,525
-[Mike] All mine.
-[reverend] In my congregation

215
00:08:44,558 --> 00:08:46,693
are railroad people
in every capacity.

216
00:08:46,727 --> 00:08:47,662
[Mike] Boy, he thinks loud.

217
00:08:47,695 --> 00:08:49,730
[reverend] And not one
who isn't safety conscious.

218
00:08:49,797 --> 00:08:51,098
[Tom] Not one who isn't drunk.

219
00:08:51,132 --> 00:08:53,667
[reverend] Accident-haters
all of us.

220
00:08:53,700 --> 00:08:55,468
Hating the waste, the pain,

221
00:08:56,403 --> 00:08:57,405
the needlessness.

222
00:08:57,438 --> 00:08:58,838
[Crow]
The filth that is humanity.

223
00:08:58,940 --> 00:09:00,775
[reverend] Yet, try as we may,

224
00:09:00,808 --> 00:09:01,708
do what we will,

225
00:09:02,877 --> 00:09:05,947
-there comes a time,
-[Tom] I'm bigger than Jesus.

226
00:09:05,980 --> 00:09:07,348
[siren blaring]

227
00:09:08,348 --> 00:09:10,652
[Mike] Oh, no. The reverend
just killed again.

228
00:09:12,320 --> 00:09:13,820
[Crow]
Ironically, the ambulance driver

229
00:09:13,853 --> 00:09:15,288
hit four people
on the way there.

230
00:09:18,825 --> 00:09:21,628
[Servo sobbing]

231
00:09:22,630 --> 00:09:24,065
[reverend] A fellow worker
hurt.

232
00:09:24,098 --> 00:09:25,667
[Mike] Get out of town,
accident lover!

233
00:09:25,667 --> 00:09:26,633
[reverend] People
never get hardened

234
00:09:26,700 --> 00:09:27,702
to a sight like this.

235
00:09:27,735 --> 00:09:29,503
[Crow]
I had an accident, too.

236
00:09:29,537 --> 00:09:31,105
[reverend] But, it isn't only
what happens

237
00:09:31,138 --> 00:09:33,340
-to the man in the ambulance.
-[Crow] No?

238
00:09:33,373 --> 00:09:34,475
[reverend]
There's another story

239
00:09:34,508 --> 00:09:37,545
-behind most accidents.
-[Tom] Communism.

240
00:09:37,578 --> 00:09:38,947
[reverend] The story,

241
00:09:38,980 --> 00:09:40,782
of what happens to the others.

242
00:09:40,848 --> 00:09:43,417
[Mike] The doctor's wife
had died, he paused to smell--

243
00:09:43,450 --> 00:09:45,620
[reverend] You might say,
I've got sort of an inside track

244
00:09:45,653 --> 00:09:47,455
when it comes
to knowing the inside story

245
00:09:47,488 --> 00:09:48,788
behind an accident.

246
00:09:49,923 --> 00:09:52,760
It strikes me that the ones
who are hurt most

247
00:09:52,793 --> 00:09:55,963
by the carelessness
that causes accidents

248
00:09:55,997 --> 00:09:58,032
are the ones
who weren't even there.

249
00:09:58,065 --> 00:10:00,802
[Crow]
I didn't hate accidents enough.

250
00:10:00,835 --> 00:10:01,702
[Tom] Shame.

251
00:10:02,737 --> 00:10:05,172
I often stopped by here
for a cup of coffee.

252
00:10:05,205 --> 00:10:06,273
[Mike]
That's really interesting.

253
00:10:06,307 --> 00:10:07,808
[reverend] Whenever I do,
I'm reminded

254
00:10:07,875 --> 00:10:08,875
of the things that happened

255
00:10:08,908 --> 00:10:10,443
to the people
who weren't even there.

256
00:10:10,477 --> 00:10:12,145
-Oh, sure-- huh?
-[Mike chuckling]

257
00:10:12,178 --> 00:10:14,148
[reverend] I re-read the story
in the sad eyes

258
00:10:14,215 --> 00:10:15,850
of the girl who serves me.

259
00:10:15,883 --> 00:10:17,885
[Tom]
And it really pisses me off.

260
00:10:17,918 --> 00:10:19,387
[Crow] Yes, I'm ruddy.

261
00:10:19,420 --> 00:10:21,388
-This is Helen.
-[Tom] Hi, Helen.

262
00:10:21,422 --> 00:10:22,790
[reverend] Although,
she has never been

263
00:10:22,823 --> 00:10:24,858
-in an accident, Helen is,
-[Tom] She acts like she has.

264
00:10:24,925 --> 00:10:27,160
nevertheless,
an accident victim.

265
00:10:27,193 --> 00:10:28,628
[Mike]
Now can I get my pancakes?

266
00:10:28,662 --> 00:10:30,698
[reverend] Her eyes were not
always sorrowful.

267
00:10:30,732 --> 00:10:32,733
Once they were bright
and shiny.

268
00:10:32,767 --> 00:10:34,102
[Crow] Her coat glossy.

269
00:10:34,135 --> 00:10:36,703
[reverend] Yes, I remember,
how those eyes used to glow

270
00:10:36,737 --> 00:10:38,705
whenever Helen
thought about Joe.

271
00:10:40,140 --> 00:10:42,108
-Joe Tendler was--
-[Tom] It's a belt sander!

272
00:10:42,142 --> 00:10:43,577
-[Servo screaming]
-[reverend] In those days,

273
00:10:43,643 --> 00:10:45,578
Joe was about as average
a young fellow

274
00:10:45,612 --> 00:10:47,382
-as you'd be likely to meet.
-[Mike] He sounds great.

275
00:10:47,415 --> 00:10:50,317
[reverend] Young, easygoing,
good company.

276
00:10:50,383 --> 00:10:51,418
[Crow] Prone to violence.

277
00:10:51,452 --> 00:10:53,553
[reverend] He hadn't a complaint
in the world.

278
00:10:53,587 --> 00:10:55,422
Well, maybe one complaint.

279
00:10:55,455 --> 00:10:57,858
He was getting a little tired
of being a bachelor.

280
00:10:57,925 --> 00:11:00,895
Eating on the run,
cleaning up your own place,

281
00:11:00,928 --> 00:11:02,697
-[Servo laughing]
-if you got around to it.

282
00:11:02,730 --> 00:11:05,332
But all that was due
for a change pretty soon.

283
00:11:05,365 --> 00:11:06,767
[Tom] He'd chosen
his new look.

284
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:08,868
[reverend] Yes,
Joe was a pretty lucky guy.

285
00:11:08,902 --> 00:11:11,872
Good job
as road electrical foreman,

286
00:11:11,905 --> 00:11:13,807
wonderful girl
who wanted nothing

287
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:16,810
in the world more than
to be Mrs. Joe.

288
00:11:16,843 --> 00:11:19,447
-[Tom] So his name is Joe Joe?
-[Mike] I guess so.

289
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:21,248
[reverend] On his way to the job
that morning,

290
00:11:21,282 --> 00:11:23,483
Joe had to stop by
to see Helen.

291
00:11:23,550 --> 00:11:24,552
There was something

292
00:11:24,585 --> 00:11:25,887
he'd forgotten to
give her last night.

293
00:11:25,953 --> 00:11:27,622
A taste of the spoiler.

294
00:11:29,657 --> 00:11:30,992
[Tom] I spilled my coffee,
it's hot!

295
00:11:31,025 --> 00:11:32,760
"In front of all these people,
behave!"

296
00:11:32,793 --> 00:11:33,760
[Mike makes noises]

297
00:11:33,793 --> 00:11:36,463
But of course, Joe was behaving.

298
00:11:36,530 --> 00:11:38,832
The was any lucky, happy,
young fellow

299
00:11:38,865 --> 00:11:41,068
about to be married
would behave.

300
00:11:41,102 --> 00:11:42,537
It was only natural.

301
00:11:43,870 --> 00:11:46,840
Joe had to take off in an hurry
to reach that job on time.

302
00:11:46,873 --> 00:11:49,410
He had a lot more women to kiss
on his route that morning.

303
00:11:49,443 --> 00:11:50,577
[reverend] And Helen,

304
00:11:50,610 --> 00:11:52,847
well, she was on the job,
and her face

305
00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:54,882
had that glad look,
I'll always remember.

306
00:11:54,948 --> 00:11:56,617
[Tom] My man is average.

307
00:11:56,650 --> 00:11:58,218
[reverend]
It made people feel good

308
00:11:58,252 --> 00:12:00,387
just watching her feeling good.

309
00:12:00,420 --> 00:12:01,788
as she went
through the motions,

310
00:12:01,822 --> 00:12:03,590
[Tom]
I'm watching you feel good.

311
00:12:03,690 --> 00:12:05,927
[reverend] Though she was quick
and sure and competent

312
00:12:05,960 --> 00:12:07,360
in everything she did,

313
00:12:07,393 --> 00:12:08,762
-She spit in the eggs.
-[reverend] She was really

314
00:12:08,828 --> 00:12:11,698
a million miles away
from that little restaurant.

315
00:12:11,732 --> 00:12:13,433
[Tom] Mr. and Mrs. Joe Toast.

316
00:12:13,467 --> 00:12:15,068
[reverend] A million miles away.

317
00:12:16,537 --> 00:12:20,640
-In a dream world, all her own,
-[Tom] Come over to this side.

318
00:12:20,673 --> 00:12:23,010
[Crow] Obey the toaster!

319
00:12:23,043 --> 00:12:25,412
[reverend]
That dream world had everything,

320
00:12:25,445 --> 00:12:27,815
a church, complete
with ministers,

321
00:12:27,848 --> 00:12:30,417
-saying the proper words.
-[Tom] You are getting sleepy.

322
00:12:30,417 --> 00:12:32,787
[reverend] I was flattered
to be a part of Helen's dream.

323
00:12:32,820 --> 00:12:33,987
-[Tom] Ew.
-[Crow] Ew.

324
00:12:34,053 --> 00:12:35,890
[reverend]
There was a ring in it,

325
00:12:35,923 --> 00:12:37,123
and of course,

326
00:12:37,157 --> 00:12:39,427
-a beautiful dress and veil.
-[Tom] For Joe.

327
00:12:39,460 --> 00:12:40,962
[reverend]
And the handsome groom.

328
00:12:41,028 --> 00:12:42,328
[Mike] And me, of course.
[chuckling]

329
00:12:42,362 --> 00:12:44,398
[reverend] And when the ceremony
was over,

330
00:12:44,432 --> 00:12:46,933
there was the first
of many, many kisses.

331
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,403
[Crow] In my version,
things went a lot further.

332
00:12:49,437 --> 00:12:52,105
[reverend] Yes, this was Helen's
very own dream.

333
00:12:52,172 --> 00:12:54,375
And she was going to make
it worth dreaming.

334
00:12:54,408 --> 00:12:56,843
[Tom] Well, I gotta get back
to my shift, honey.

335
00:12:56,910 --> 00:12:58,845
[reverend] She wasn't skimping
on anything.

336
00:12:58,912 --> 00:13:00,747
[Mike]
She dreamed of tract housing.

337
00:13:00,780 --> 00:13:01,815
[reverend]
There was that perfectly,

338
00:13:01,882 --> 00:13:03,083
scrumptious model house

339
00:13:03,117 --> 00:13:04,852
she'd been wanting
for all these years.

340
00:13:04,885 --> 00:13:06,787
-[Crow] Squalor contemporary.
-[reverend] There it was,

341
00:13:06,853 --> 00:13:08,855
exactly as she'd imagined.

342
00:13:08,888 --> 00:13:11,892
And here they were,
what was the sense in waiting.

343
00:13:11,925 --> 00:13:13,827
[Tom]
Plan Five from Outer Space .

344
00:13:13,860 --> 00:13:16,663
[reverend]
The fastest sale on record.

345
00:13:16,697 --> 00:13:19,633
And while she was dreaming,
she'd furnished that house

346
00:13:19,667 --> 00:13:21,035
that very afternoon.

347
00:13:21,068 --> 00:13:22,970
So Joe could read
his evening paper

348
00:13:23,003 --> 00:13:24,872
on that pretty couch
that very night.

349
00:13:24,938 --> 00:13:26,407
-[Mike] Where's my damn dinner?
-[reverend] Where she could take

350
00:13:26,440 --> 00:13:28,442
that paper away
and cuddle close.

351
00:13:28,508 --> 00:13:29,443
[Tom] Ooh.

352
00:13:29,510 --> 00:13:30,878
[reverend] She'd dreamed
of everything

353
00:13:30,912 --> 00:13:33,213
any dreamy young girl
had ever dreamed before.

354
00:13:33,280 --> 00:13:34,582
[Tom] They're in Hell!

355
00:13:34,615 --> 00:13:38,152
[reverend] Of crackling fires,
of dimmed lights,

356
00:13:38,185 --> 00:13:39,353
of love.

357
00:13:39,387 --> 00:13:41,055
[Crow] They should've
had a train scene here.

358
00:13:41,088 --> 00:13:42,155
[reverend]
And even in her dreams,

359
00:13:42,222 --> 00:13:44,023
Helen missed Joe
when he was gone.

360
00:13:44,057 --> 00:13:45,960
[Mike]
That was so incredibly average.

361
00:13:46,027 --> 00:13:47,327
[reverend]
She was a clever dreamer,

362
00:13:47,393 --> 00:13:48,695
[baby cooing]

363
00:13:48,762 --> 00:13:50,965
so she arranged for little Joe
to be there to keep her

364
00:13:50,998 --> 00:13:52,867
from getting lonesome
when daddy was away.

365
00:13:52,933 --> 00:13:54,167
[Crow] Speaking of accidents.

366
00:13:54,202 --> 00:13:55,735
[reverend]
Beginning of a new life,

367
00:13:56,570 --> 00:13:57,772
but the end of a dream.

368
00:13:58,705 --> 00:14:01,608
[Tom] Okay. Diaper
the toast and butter the baby.

369
00:14:05,078 --> 00:14:06,980
[Servo buzzing]

370
00:14:07,047 --> 00:14:09,950
[reverend] But on the job late
in the afternoon of that day,

371
00:14:09,983 --> 00:14:13,320
the real-life Joe
was in a real live hurry.

372
00:14:13,353 --> 00:14:15,722
He hadn't seen his Helen
since early that morning.

373
00:14:15,788 --> 00:14:17,390
[Tom singing]
♪ Wichita lineman ♪

374
00:14:17,423 --> 00:14:20,560
[reverend] "C'mon, you guys.
Get a move on.

375
00:14:20,593 --> 00:14:21,828
Let's get out of here."

376
00:14:21,862 --> 00:14:23,330
[Crow]
He might've been heard to say.

377
00:14:23,363 --> 00:14:24,832
[reverend]
To the impatient Joe,

378
00:14:24,865 --> 00:14:27,033
the men seemed almost
purposely stalling

379
00:14:27,067 --> 00:14:28,502
as they stowed their gear.

380
00:14:28,535 --> 00:14:30,703
[Tom] Joe smelled conspiracy
in everything.

381
00:14:32,973 --> 00:14:36,510
[reverend] And now, the wheels
of fate were set in motion.

382
00:14:36,543 --> 00:14:40,080
Call them the wheels of fate,
or the wheels of chance,

383
00:14:40,113 --> 00:14:42,215
-[Tom] Or call them Ernie.
-[reverend] but consider,

384
00:14:42,248 --> 00:14:44,618
the man of the wheel
was in the driver's seat.

385
00:14:44,652 --> 00:14:46,053
[Tom]
Any case it involved a wheel.

386
00:14:46,087 --> 00:14:47,520
[reverend] It was Joe
who was calling

387
00:14:47,588 --> 00:14:48,622
the turn of the wheel.

388
00:14:48,655 --> 00:14:50,057
[Mike]
Joe is the king of the world.

389
00:14:50,090 --> 00:14:51,992
[reverend]
They went through a stop sign,

390
00:14:52,025 --> 00:14:53,560
Better take it easy, Joe.

391
00:14:53,593 --> 00:14:55,828
[Crow]
Get out of my head, reverend!

392
00:14:55,862 --> 00:14:58,065
[music playing]

393
00:14:59,333 --> 00:15:02,770
[Tom] Joe's thirst for danger
seems quite reasonable.

394
00:15:02,803 --> 00:15:06,373
[reverend] The others,
they were along for the ride,

395
00:15:06,373 --> 00:15:08,542
whether they liked it or not.

396
00:15:08,575 --> 00:15:10,843
Liked it? They hated it.

397
00:15:10,877 --> 00:15:13,147
-[Tom] A flag on the moon.
-[reverend] What could they do?

398
00:15:13,213 --> 00:15:14,615
They were trapped.

399
00:15:15,415 --> 00:15:17,283
Yes, Joe had a lady,

400
00:15:17,317 --> 00:15:19,953
and he just couldn't get
there fast enough.

401
00:15:19,987 --> 00:15:21,822
[Tom] For those two scoops
of raisins,

402
00:15:21,855 --> 00:15:23,990
[tires squealing]

403
00:15:24,558 --> 00:15:26,427
[Tom] Oh, not again, Joe!

404
00:15:27,527 --> 00:15:30,530
[Crow] That's your standard
crash Foley.

405
00:15:30,563 --> 00:15:32,298
[reverend]
Now, it was payoff time.

406
00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:35,068
One, still able to move.

407
00:15:36,170 --> 00:15:38,272
Two, still able to move.

408
00:15:39,072 --> 00:15:40,907
They had escaped the trap.

409
00:15:40,940 --> 00:15:42,475
[Tom]
It was an average accident.

410
00:15:42,508 --> 00:15:45,078
[reverend] Their threescore
and ten were still before them.

411
00:15:45,112 --> 00:15:46,613
[Mike] Give or take a score.

412
00:15:46,647 --> 00:15:49,082
[reverend]
But the trap had sprung on Joe.

413
00:15:49,115 --> 00:15:51,585
-[Crow] Serve 'ya right.
-[reverend] Yes.

414
00:15:51,618 --> 00:15:53,587
Now, it was payoff time.

415
00:15:53,620 --> 00:15:55,555
[Tom]
Such are the wages of sex.

416
00:15:55,588 --> 00:15:57,357
[reverend]
Easy going Joe Tendler,

417
00:15:57,390 --> 00:15:58,992
[Mike]
Was trapped in his own juicer.

418
00:16:00,160 --> 00:16:02,128
Joe became
an avant-garde composer.

419
00:16:02,162 --> 00:16:03,730
[reverend] After a year
in that neck brace,

420
00:16:03,763 --> 00:16:05,298
Joe wasn't quite
such good company

421
00:16:05,332 --> 00:16:06,133
as he used to be.

422
00:16:07,100 --> 00:16:10,070
But the Word said,
for better or for worse,

423
00:16:10,103 --> 00:16:11,638
in sickness or in health,

424
00:16:11,672 --> 00:16:13,107
[Tom]
Do you take this bionic man?

425
00:16:13,140 --> 00:16:15,308
[reverend]
Sure, Jo tried to call it off,

426
00:16:15,342 --> 00:16:18,078
but a girl
like Helen doesn't run out.

427
00:16:18,112 --> 00:16:19,380
There was quite a difference

428
00:16:19,413 --> 00:16:22,282
between Helen's dream
and the reality,

429
00:16:22,348 --> 00:16:23,750
there'd be quite a difference

430
00:16:23,783 --> 00:16:25,985
between living
with the dream Joe

431
00:16:26,053 --> 00:16:27,520
and the real one.

432
00:16:27,553 --> 00:16:29,690
[Crow] Their honeymoon at Six
Flags is going to be real fun.

433
00:16:33,160 --> 00:16:36,197
-[Tom] Hey, Joe.
-[Servo choking and yelling]

434
00:16:36,263 --> 00:16:38,132
[reverend]
It needn't have happened at all.

435
00:16:38,165 --> 00:16:39,533
[Tom] You could've married me.

436
00:16:40,867 --> 00:16:44,337
[Mike] Look at this button I
found in the collection plate.

437
00:16:44,405 --> 00:16:49,610
[theme music playing]

438
00:16:52,012 --> 00:16:54,047
[reverend] There are many days
in the years left for Joe,

439
00:16:54,113 --> 00:16:55,215
[Mike] Too many.

440
00:16:55,248 --> 00:16:57,583
[reverend] For George Price,
there are fewer.

441
00:16:57,617 --> 00:16:58,918
[Tom] Hey, you dead yet?

442
00:16:58,952 --> 00:17:00,920
[reverend] And the days
George does have left,

443
00:17:00,953 --> 00:17:02,088
are embittered days.

444
00:17:02,122 --> 00:17:04,257
-[Crow scoffing]
-[reverend] Flavorless.

445
00:17:04,290 --> 00:17:05,125
Futile.

446
00:17:05,158 --> 00:17:07,360
[Crow] Get off my land,
you pious son of a--

447
00:17:07,393 --> 00:17:10,230
[reverend] To George, the sound
of that approaching car

448
00:17:10,263 --> 00:17:13,133
as it nears the house
across the street,

449
00:17:13,200 --> 00:17:17,270
prods back into his memory,
a scene he has fought to erase,

450
00:17:17,303 --> 00:17:18,838
[Mike] But then so does
every other sound.

451
00:17:18,905 --> 00:17:20,240
[reverend]
The scene he cannot forget.

452
00:17:20,273 --> 00:17:22,142
[Tom] He's having a great day.

453
00:17:22,175 --> 00:17:25,712
[reverend] This is Lenny Bellows
the boy across the street.

454
00:17:25,745 --> 00:17:28,948
The boy who once
was like a son to George.

455
00:17:28,982 --> 00:17:31,017
Just across the street.

456
00:17:31,085 --> 00:17:34,455
Where everyday, George can't
possibly help seeing him.

457
00:17:34,488 --> 00:17:36,257
[Crow] Better go buy
some salt pellets.

458
00:17:36,290 --> 00:17:38,558
[reverend] Everyday,
George remembers.

459
00:17:38,592 --> 00:17:40,760
[Mike] When he was good friends
with Dean Rusk.

460
00:17:40,827 --> 00:17:43,030
[reverend] George remembers
how it was in the happy days,

461
00:17:43,063 --> 00:17:44,798
with the Price
and Bellows families.

462
00:17:44,832 --> 00:17:45,898
[Tom] Eating sandwiches.

463
00:17:45,965 --> 00:17:48,168
[reverend] Fred Bellows,
Lenny's father, and George,

464
00:17:48,202 --> 00:17:49,470
good neighbors,
good friends.

465
00:17:49,537 --> 00:17:50,970
[Tom] And Michelob.

466
00:17:51,003 --> 00:17:52,840
[reverend] They'd spend hours
figuring the fun they'd have

467
00:17:52,873 --> 00:17:54,908
-on their retirement pay.
-[all cheering]

468
00:17:54,942 --> 00:17:56,577
The trips they'd take together.

469
00:17:56,610 --> 00:17:58,712
The wonderful places
they'd see.

470
00:17:59,547 --> 00:18:00,913
It would be soon now.

471
00:18:00,947 --> 00:18:02,783
[Crow]
Let's go on a Hawaiian sex tour.

472
00:18:02,817 --> 00:18:04,083
[reverend]
Their wives had waited patiently

473
00:18:04,117 --> 00:18:07,320
through all the long years
of railroad service.

474
00:18:07,353 --> 00:18:08,288
-[Tom] Tony Dow!
-[reverend] Lenny would go

475
00:18:08,322 --> 00:18:11,225
to college. It was all planned.

476
00:18:11,225 --> 00:18:14,060
There wasn't a cloud
in the bright, blue sky

477
00:18:14,093 --> 00:18:16,230
-of their future.
-[Mike] Then, I showed up.

478
00:18:16,263 --> 00:18:18,032
[Servo chuckling]

479
00:18:18,065 --> 00:18:21,468
[reverend] Forty-two years
of good, honest work.

480
00:18:21,535 --> 00:18:23,437
George was proud of those years.

481
00:18:23,470 --> 00:18:25,272
-[Tom] George.
-Proud of the strength.

482
00:18:25,338 --> 00:18:27,740
and stamina
that had kept him going.

483
00:18:27,773 --> 00:18:31,312
But today, somehow,
he didn't feel so good.

484
00:18:31,345 --> 00:18:33,713
And when it was time
to go back to work,

485
00:18:33,747 --> 00:18:36,283
George wondered whether he
was going to be able to make it.

486
00:18:36,317 --> 00:18:37,683
[Crow] George, you're hilarious.

487
00:18:37,750 --> 00:18:39,252
[reverend]
Funny, these last few weeks

488
00:18:39,285 --> 00:18:40,620
he hadn't been
quite up to snuff.

489
00:18:40,653 --> 00:18:42,188
[Tom] Diarrhea is like a stone.

490
00:18:42,222 --> 00:18:44,592
[reverend] Nobody noticed,
he hadn't mentioned it,

491
00:18:44,625 --> 00:18:47,093
-probably just indigestion.
-[Mike] Hey,

492
00:18:47,160 --> 00:18:48,895
have a massive coronary
on your own time.

493
00:18:48,962 --> 00:18:49,763
[reverend] He'll be alright.

494
00:18:50,863 --> 00:18:53,733
If he could only hold out
until retirement time.

495
00:18:53,767 --> 00:18:55,235
[Crow] It's at
5 o'clock today.

496
00:18:56,403 --> 00:18:58,305
[reverend]
But now he had a job to do.

497
00:18:58,338 --> 00:18:59,707
And nobody
was going to say

498
00:18:59,740 --> 00:19:01,708
George Price gave up
on the job.

499
00:19:01,742 --> 00:19:04,277
[Tom] George Price gave up
on the job. There, I said it.

500
00:19:04,277 --> 00:19:05,845
[reverend]
And so, as Fred Bellows

501
00:19:05,878 --> 00:19:07,480
climbed up to go back
to his job,

502
00:19:08,282 --> 00:19:09,883
George went back to his.

503
00:19:09,917 --> 00:19:11,252
[Mike]
Robust and cheerful as ever.

504
00:19:11,285 --> 00:19:13,253
[reverend]
A small voice whispered,

505
00:19:13,287 --> 00:19:15,688
"Report sick. Get a relief man.

506
00:19:15,722 --> 00:19:18,292
-Don't be a fool, George".
-[Crow] Kill your parents.

507
00:19:18,358 --> 00:19:19,860
[reverend]
But George had a job to do,

508
00:19:19,893 --> 00:19:21,462
and he was going to stick to it.

509
00:19:21,495 --> 00:19:23,063
[Mike] Boo Radley waved him on.

510
00:19:23,930 --> 00:19:25,665
[reverend] So, there was George

511
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:29,135
and Fred,

512
00:19:29,168 --> 00:19:30,403
and Bill said,

513
00:19:30,403 --> 00:19:32,172
"C'mon, easy."

514
00:19:32,205 --> 00:19:34,240
[Crow]
They called him the "derailer".

515
00:19:37,310 --> 00:19:39,212
[Mike] Well,
what's with Georgey Worgey?

516
00:19:40,447 --> 00:19:41,047
[music playing]

517
00:19:42,148 --> 00:19:44,517
[Tom] Shouldn't have thought
of Mamie Eisenhower.

518
00:19:44,585 --> 00:19:46,887
[music playing]

519
00:19:47,988 --> 00:19:50,357
[Tom] Uh, George,
if this is one of your pranks...

520
00:19:50,390 --> 00:19:51,458
[Servo chuckling]

521
00:19:54,662 --> 00:19:55,895
[Mike] Oh, this is terrible.

522
00:19:55,928 --> 00:19:58,065
He died minutes
before his time.

523
00:19:59,398 --> 00:20:00,867
[Tom] Good one, George.

524
00:20:04,270 --> 00:20:06,773
[reverend] George tried to go
to Fred Bellows funeral...

525
00:20:06,840 --> 00:20:08,108
[Crow] But Baywatch was on.

526
00:20:08,142 --> 00:20:09,442
[reverend]
...but the doctor said no.

527
00:20:10,443 --> 00:20:13,347
You don't walk around two days
after a heart attack.

528
00:20:13,413 --> 00:20:15,182
[Mike] Why couldn't he have
killed the guy two doors down?

529
00:20:15,215 --> 00:20:16,917
[reverend] But it couldn't keep
him away from the window.

530
00:20:18,318 --> 00:20:20,153
He watched Grace and Lenny,

531
00:20:20,187 --> 00:20:21,988
-[Tom] And Squiggy.
-[reverend] Lenny,

532
00:20:22,022 --> 00:20:24,257
who wouldn't
be going to college this fall...

533
00:20:24,323 --> 00:20:26,327
-Because he's dumb.
-[reverend] ...or any fall.

534
00:20:26,360 --> 00:20:27,627
[Tom] Can you bring me back
a program?

535
00:20:27,693 --> 00:20:29,228
[reverend]
Grace, who faced a future

536
00:20:29,295 --> 00:20:31,130
of grief and loneliness,

537
00:20:31,163 --> 00:20:32,298
[Tom] Boring.

538
00:20:33,333 --> 00:20:35,235
[Crow]
There. My problem went away.

539
00:20:37,903 --> 00:20:39,907
[Mike] Alright. Let's get
this guy planted.

540
00:20:41,308 --> 00:20:43,643
[Crow] They'll circle George's
house a few times,

541
00:20:43,677 --> 00:20:44,912
then go on to the cemetery.

542
00:20:45,878 --> 00:20:47,982
[reverend]
That was over three years ago.

543
00:20:48,015 --> 00:20:49,382
[Tom] George hasn't moved.

544
00:20:49,448 --> 00:20:52,452
[reverend] George still can't
get around very well.

545
00:20:52,518 --> 00:20:55,888
Lenny Bellows still lives
across the street.

546
00:20:55,922 --> 00:20:59,358
And though he has long
since forgiven George,

547
00:20:59,392 --> 00:21:02,728
yet his simplest actions
have become, to George,

548
00:21:02,762 --> 00:21:05,298
signs of rejection and hatred.

549
00:21:05,332 --> 00:21:06,900
[Mike] You know,
staring off into space

550
00:21:06,933 --> 00:21:09,068
hasn't been as exciting
as I thought it would be.

551
00:21:09,102 --> 00:21:12,105
[reverend] There George Price
was doomed to stay,

552
00:21:12,138 --> 00:21:15,875
across a little street,
a thousand miles wide.

553
00:21:15,875 --> 00:21:18,145
[Crow] Now, let's get
to the dismemberment.

554
00:21:19,278 --> 00:21:20,380
[reverend] As I said before,

555
00:21:20,413 --> 00:21:22,782
most of my congregation
are railroad people.

556
00:21:22,815 --> 00:21:24,517
I know the road does everything

557
00:21:24,550 --> 00:21:26,820
in its power
to prevent accidents.

558
00:21:26,887 --> 00:21:28,022
To see that equipment

559
00:21:28,088 --> 00:21:30,390
and working procedures
in the shops are as safe

560
00:21:30,423 --> 00:21:32,358
as it's possible to make them.

561
00:21:32,392 --> 00:21:35,362
And by constant reminders
to keep the men aware

562
00:21:35,395 --> 00:21:37,363
of the life
and death importance

563
00:21:37,430 --> 00:21:39,365
of observing safety rules.

564
00:21:39,432 --> 00:21:41,368
[Tom]
See our elaborate safety system?

565
00:21:41,435 --> 00:21:42,468
[Servo chuckling]

566
00:21:42,502 --> 00:21:43,837
[reverend] Yet, still they come.

567
00:21:43,870 --> 00:21:46,005
-[Mike] This is Needle Park.
-[reverend] The ones who forget

568
00:21:46,038 --> 00:21:48,408
even for the barest fraction
of a second,

569
00:21:48,442 --> 00:21:50,243
that with safety,

570
00:21:50,277 --> 00:21:51,978
there are no second chances.

571
00:21:52,012 --> 00:21:54,413
[Crow]
Oh, well. Who needs fingers?

572
00:21:54,447 --> 00:21:56,517
[reverend] Not all trips
to the hospital, though,

573
00:21:56,550 --> 00:21:58,918
-are necessarily disastrous.
-[Tom] Just mine.

574
00:21:58,952 --> 00:22:00,187
[reverend]
For example, there was the trip

575
00:22:00,220 --> 00:22:02,522
Charlie O'Neil made
with his wife.

576
00:22:02,555 --> 00:22:04,457
That was about
the happiest trip

577
00:22:04,490 --> 00:22:05,725
Charlie ever took.

578
00:22:05,758 --> 00:22:08,828
He and Sue had been
looking forward to it for years.

579
00:22:08,828 --> 00:22:10,497
Waiting. Hoping.

580
00:22:10,530 --> 00:22:12,665
-[Crow] For a pap smear?
-[reverend] Finally it happened.

581
00:22:12,698 --> 00:22:16,302
There'd be a new little O'Neil
along any minute now.

582
00:22:16,370 --> 00:22:19,038
Charlie was so happy
he forgot everything else.

583
00:22:19,072 --> 00:22:21,442
-[Servo chuckling]
-Well, almost everything.

584
00:22:21,475 --> 00:22:24,343
[Mike] He almost forgot
his ventriloquial figure.

585
00:22:24,377 --> 00:22:25,745
[Servo laughing]

586
00:22:25,778 --> 00:22:27,113
[reverend] He didn't quite
believe them a little later

587
00:22:27,147 --> 00:22:28,882
when they told him
it might take a while

588
00:22:28,915 --> 00:22:30,750
and he might as well go back
to work.

589
00:22:30,783 --> 00:22:33,487
[Tom] This has nothing
to do with you. Go on.

590
00:22:33,520 --> 00:22:34,855
[music playing]

591
00:22:35,655 --> 00:22:37,123
[reverend] On the way
to the shop,

592
00:22:37,157 --> 00:22:39,492
-he remembered something.
-[Crow] He had to go kiss Helen.

593
00:22:39,525 --> 00:22:41,127
[reverend]
It was just about the last thing

594
00:22:41,193 --> 00:22:42,528
he did remember that day,

595
00:22:43,263 --> 00:22:44,430
Cigars.

596
00:22:44,463 --> 00:22:46,433
[Mike] He was going
to Ernie Kovacs' house.

597
00:22:46,467 --> 00:22:48,468
[reverend] They weren't
going to catch Charlie off-base

598
00:22:48,502 --> 00:22:49,803
when the big news came.

599
00:22:49,837 --> 00:22:52,005
[Crow] Charlie was close now.
You could smell him.

600
00:22:54,207 --> 00:22:55,642
[reverend] He hoped it wouldn't
be too long

601
00:22:55,708 --> 00:22:58,145
-before he could break them out.
-[Tom] So he disrobed.

602
00:22:59,913 --> 00:23:01,582
[reverend]
There was a phone call coming,

603
00:23:01,582 --> 00:23:03,517
he told the Foreman, a big one.

604
00:23:03,550 --> 00:23:05,718
Please get it to him fast
when it came.

605
00:23:05,752 --> 00:23:07,520
[Crow] Ah, get back to work.

606
00:23:07,553 --> 00:23:10,023
[reverend] Charlie did remember
one more thing that day.

607
00:23:10,057 --> 00:23:12,525
To take proper care
getting Bill's attention.

608
00:23:13,593 --> 00:23:15,628
Gentle pressure
on the shoulders

609
00:23:15,662 --> 00:23:16,697
so as to not startle him,

610
00:23:16,763 --> 00:23:18,398
[Mike]
And a soft kiss on the neck.

611
00:23:18,432 --> 00:23:20,267
[reverend] That hissing,
open flame

612
00:23:20,333 --> 00:23:21,735
is deadlier than
any snake.

613
00:23:22,535 --> 00:23:23,370
Charlie knew it.

614
00:23:24,237 --> 00:23:27,273
Every man who works
a torch knows it.

615
00:23:27,307 --> 00:23:29,542
When you come near em,
you come up smooth.

616
00:23:29,575 --> 00:23:31,445
You don't make
any sudden motions.

617
00:23:31,478 --> 00:23:32,878
[Tom]
I have no idea what I'm doing.

618
00:23:32,912 --> 00:23:33,447
[Servo chuckling]

619
00:23:34,647 --> 00:23:36,850
[Crow] Picasso's woman.
Looks like a baboon!

620
00:23:41,087 --> 00:23:43,790
[reverend] This was it.
a phone call for Charlie.

621
00:23:43,823 --> 00:23:46,058
[Mike] Charlie, does fur run
in your family?

622
00:23:47,993 --> 00:23:50,063
[Tom] Someone brought donuts!

623
00:23:53,800 --> 00:23:56,202
[reverend] Yes. Yes.

624
00:23:56,235 --> 00:23:59,072
A boy. Nine pounds. Wow.

625
00:23:59,105 --> 00:24:01,407
[Crow]
I've got great sperm!

626
00:24:01,475 --> 00:24:02,675
[reverend]
You can forgive Charlie

627
00:24:02,708 --> 00:24:04,777
for being excited.
Who wouldn't be?

628
00:24:04,845 --> 00:24:06,178
A newborn father is one

629
00:24:06,245 --> 00:24:08,515
of the most excitable people
in the whole world.

630
00:24:08,548 --> 00:24:10,517
[Crow] Other than the father
at the time of conception.

631
00:24:12,618 --> 00:24:13,787
[reverend]
You can forgive Charlie

632
00:24:13,820 --> 00:24:16,490
for passing out cigars
during work hours

633
00:24:16,523 --> 00:24:18,625
even though he knew
he should've waited 'til lunch.

634
00:24:18,658 --> 00:24:19,960
[Tom]
He should've waited 'til lunch.

635
00:24:20,027 --> 00:24:21,460
[Mike] Ted's awful.

636
00:24:21,528 --> 00:24:22,695
[reverend]
You can even forgive Charlie

637
00:24:22,728 --> 00:24:24,397
for what happened next.

638
00:24:24,430 --> 00:24:27,967
For that one instant
of final, fatal forgetting.

639
00:24:30,103 --> 00:24:31,370
[Tom] Alan Arkin!

640
00:24:34,875 --> 00:24:37,377
[Crow] Why am I welding?
Why would I be welding to this?

641
00:24:37,443 --> 00:24:38,945
I don't no
understand.

642
00:24:39,012 --> 00:24:40,880
[all screaming]

643
00:24:40,913 --> 00:24:42,748
[Mike]
You don't have to hold it there!

644
00:24:42,815 --> 00:24:44,183
[Crow] Hold the phone.

645
00:24:46,118 --> 00:24:47,787
[Crow] Oh, now
he's T-Bone Burnett.

646
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:49,923
[reverend] You can forgive
Charlie for everything.

647
00:24:49,957 --> 00:24:51,758
And you can understand
how it happened,

648
00:24:51,792 --> 00:24:53,260
[Tom] Yet I want him punished.

649
00:24:53,327 --> 00:24:54,895
[reverend]
How one thing you cannot do.

650
00:24:54,928 --> 00:24:56,128
[Mike]
Put your elbow in your ear?

651
00:24:56,162 --> 00:24:57,730
[reverend] You cannot change it.

652
00:24:57,763 --> 00:24:59,065
It is done.

653
00:25:00,100 --> 00:25:02,002
And there is nothing
you can say to Charlie,

654
00:25:02,068 --> 00:25:03,570
[Crow] Except gentle pressure.

655
00:25:03,603 --> 00:25:05,772
[reverend] He has said it
all to himself, many times.

656
00:25:05,838 --> 00:25:07,440
[Tom] Here, nice puppy.
Here we go.

657
00:25:07,473 --> 00:25:09,475
[reverend] He has said
that he was careless.

658
00:25:09,508 --> 00:25:11,878
that it could've been avoided,
that he knew better.

659
00:25:11,912 --> 00:25:13,078
[Mike] Here's your bottle.

660
00:25:13,078 --> 00:25:15,582
[reverend] That he only forgot
for a second.

661
00:25:15,615 --> 00:25:17,550
One tiny second.

662
00:25:18,452 --> 00:25:21,420
And in every case,
Charlie is right.

663
00:25:22,922 --> 00:25:24,590
But, still the thing is done.

664
00:25:24,623 --> 00:25:26,492
[Crow] I'm over the fence, man.
I'm outta here!

665
00:25:26,525 --> 00:25:28,027
Charlie has never seen
his baby son

666
00:25:28,093 --> 00:25:29,662
[Tom]
Or the five Mrs. Buchanans.

667
00:25:29,695 --> 00:25:31,263
[reverend]
and Charlie never will.

668
00:25:31,297 --> 00:25:32,365
[Mike] So the leading causes

669
00:25:32,398 --> 00:25:35,035
of accidents are joy,
sex and old age?

670
00:25:35,068 --> 00:25:37,370
[reverend] It is written
in the Old Testament,

671
00:25:37,437 --> 00:25:39,805
to each of us,
this allotment of years,

672
00:25:39,838 --> 00:25:41,842
three score and ten.

673
00:25:41,875 --> 00:25:43,577
[Crow] And there's a psalm
about welding.

674
00:25:43,610 --> 00:25:45,445
[reverend] When you add
that up literally,

675
00:25:45,478 --> 00:25:51,652
it says that the days
of our years number 25,567.

676
00:25:51,718 --> 00:25:53,953
A trivial accident
might take away one

677
00:25:53,987 --> 00:25:55,722
or two of those days.

678
00:25:55,755 --> 00:25:59,025
A serious one might take away
a hundred or a thousand.

679
00:25:59,092 --> 00:26:01,060
[Mike] A disastrous one
would be cool to watch.

680
00:26:01,093 --> 00:26:03,597
[reverend] Suddenly, there might
be no days left at all.

681
00:26:03,663 --> 00:26:05,798
[Tom] America's favorite
deacon of death.

682
00:26:05,832 --> 00:26:09,035
[Crow] Hey! We-- we're trying
to film here!

683
00:26:09,068 --> 00:26:10,703
[reverend]
The Lord has allotted them.

684
00:26:11,638 --> 00:26:14,173
Let not man
by his thoughtlessness

685
00:26:14,207 --> 00:26:16,643
diminish the blessings
of the Lord.

686
00:26:16,677 --> 00:26:18,745
[Mike] Ironically, the reverend
was maimed and crippled

687
00:26:18,778 --> 00:26:20,280
giving communion.

688
00:26:20,347 --> 00:26:22,148
[Tom] Reverend, I'm having a
crisis of faith.

689
00:26:22,182 --> 00:26:23,015
What should I do?

690
00:26:23,048 --> 00:26:25,818
[Crow]
Get safety goggles, my son.

691
00:26:25,852 --> 00:26:28,588
[Mike] Remember to worship at
the railroad of your choice.

692
00:26:30,890 --> 00:26:32,692
[doors closing]

693
00:26:43,235 --> 00:26:46,305
You know, that-- that short
had a interesting juxtaposition.

694
00:26:46,372 --> 00:26:49,775
On the one hand, got lots
of valuable safety information,

695
00:26:49,808 --> 00:26:51,077
-Sure.
-and on the other hand,

696
00:26:51,143 --> 00:26:52,578
it made me want to kill myself.

697
00:26:52,612 --> 00:26:54,413
[laughing]

698
00:26:54,447 --> 00:26:55,748
But you-- you've taken

699
00:26:55,782 --> 00:26:57,117
that information to heart,
haven't you?

700
00:26:57,117 --> 00:26:58,718
Oh-- oh, that's--
that's right, Mike.

701
00:26:58,752 --> 00:27:01,755
Uh-- uh, what was that thing
I was supposed to remember?

702
00:27:01,788 --> 00:27:03,723
Uh, Gentile pray-sure,

703
00:27:03,757 --> 00:27:05,225
gentleben, gentle on my--

704
00:27:05,258 --> 00:27:07,860
No. No. No.
Gentle pressure.

705
00:27:07,893 --> 00:27:09,528
-Gentle pressure.
-Gentle, Gentle pressure.

706
00:27:09,595 --> 00:27:10,697
Can't stress that enough.

707
00:27:11,865 --> 00:27:14,867
Gentle pressure.
I got it! Gentle pressure.

708
00:27:14,900 --> 00:27:17,237
That's right. Gentle pressure.
Now, as it happens,

709
00:27:17,270 --> 00:27:18,838
uh, Tom Servo is doing

710
00:27:18,872 --> 00:27:20,840
some welding on the other side
of this show.

711
00:27:20,873 --> 00:27:23,075
As it happens,
completely coincidentally,

712
00:27:23,142 --> 00:27:25,778
so go over and get
his attention, using

713
00:27:25,812 --> 00:27:27,780
-gentle pressure.
-Gentle pressure.

714
00:27:27,813 --> 00:27:28,782
Don't worry, Mike.

715
00:27:28,848 --> 00:27:29,748
[laughing]

716
00:27:29,782 --> 00:27:31,383
I won't forget that.

717
00:27:32,718 --> 00:27:35,288
Gentle pressure.
Gentle pressure.

718
00:27:35,322 --> 00:27:36,622
[Crow] Hey, Servo, how's you--

719
00:27:36,655 --> 00:27:39,725
[Crow screaming]

720
00:27:41,493 --> 00:27:42,828
Gentle what, Mike?

721
00:27:43,997 --> 00:27:45,565
Crow T. Robot.

722
00:27:45,598 --> 00:27:47,700
He's always getting
into one scrape after another.

723
00:27:50,370 --> 00:27:54,107
[theme music playing]

724
00:27:58,778 --> 00:28:01,848
-[Tom] I'm sorry, Crow.
-[Crow] It happens.

725
00:28:01,882 --> 00:28:04,750
[music playing]

726
00:28:05,752 --> 00:28:07,753
[Tom] The Untouchables!

727
00:28:07,820 --> 00:28:08,822
[music playing]

728
00:28:09,688 --> 00:28:12,325
[Tom]
MCP, Most Crappiest Productions.

729
00:28:12,392 --> 00:28:13,993
[laughing]

730
00:28:14,027 --> 00:28:16,195
[Crow] I hope there's more
accidents in this movie.

731
00:28:18,363 --> 00:28:20,833
[Tom] Ladies and Gentlemen,
Mitzi Gaynor!

732
00:28:23,837 --> 00:28:25,003
[siren blaring]

733
00:28:25,037 --> 00:28:28,073
-[Crow] Another accident!
-[Tom] Oh, no.

734
00:28:28,107 --> 00:28:29,808
[gunfire]

735
00:28:29,875 --> 00:28:31,878
[Mike] Newt Gingrich's America.

736
00:28:33,547 --> 00:28:35,648
[Tom] That's one stressful
Hollywood premier.

737
00:28:37,050 --> 00:28:40,018
[Crow] The family Von Trapp.
[mimicing gunfire]

738
00:28:41,087 --> 00:28:43,423
-[shots firing]
-[sirens blaring]

739
00:28:43,423 --> 00:28:46,025
[Mike] Yes. Yes, yes.

740
00:28:46,058 --> 00:28:49,762
[Crow]
-Zing, -rent, -ent...

741
00:28:50,730 --> 00:28:52,998
[Mike]
Am I that amazingly transparent?

742
00:28:53,032 --> 00:28:54,500
[all laughing]

743
00:28:56,535 --> 00:28:57,937
[Crow]
The names have been changed

744
00:28:57,970 --> 00:28:59,738
to protect the visible.

745
00:29:01,407 --> 00:29:03,777
[Tom]
How can a woman be Kevin Kelly!

746
00:29:05,145 --> 00:29:06,847
[Mike] Oh, no. Cops.

747
00:29:08,915 --> 00:29:10,850
[Crow] There are signs of
water damage on the credits.

748
00:29:13,487 --> 00:29:18,525
[Tom] ♪ Here we come, with our
movie, with the screenplay ♪

749
00:29:18,558 --> 00:29:19,958
by Jack Lewis...

750
00:29:20,960 --> 00:29:23,897
[Mike] Music calks
the scenes together, ca--

751
00:29:23,930 --> 00:29:25,898
-Calker.
-[Tom] Yes.

752
00:29:25,932 --> 00:29:27,233
-[Mike] It's calks.
-[Tom] Yes.

753
00:29:27,300 --> 00:29:29,902
[Crow] I'm glad it's not
the annoying transparent man.

754
00:29:29,935 --> 00:29:31,303
[all laughing]

755
00:29:32,138 --> 00:29:33,973
[Tom] Credits background
by Formica,

756
00:29:34,040 --> 00:29:35,242
the laminate of the future.

757
00:29:39,778 --> 00:29:42,248
Hey, it's got
photographic effects.

758
00:29:42,282 --> 00:29:44,417
[Mike] What they mean
by photographic effects

759
00:29:44,450 --> 00:29:45,685
is that they filmed it.

760
00:29:45,752 --> 00:29:47,053
-[Crow]Ah!
-[Tom] Ah!

761
00:29:48,153 --> 00:29:51,457
[Mike] Ah, nobody did Hamlet
like Lester Guthrie.

762
00:29:51,523 --> 00:29:53,927
[Tom] This is a modern dress
amazing transparent.

763
00:29:59,565 --> 00:30:02,268
[Crow] And now, William Wegman's
Mein Kampf.

764
00:30:02,268 --> 00:30:03,268
[Servo panting]

765
00:30:03,302 --> 00:30:05,003
[Tom] I'm really a cat person.

766
00:30:08,073 --> 00:30:09,875
-[Mike] Hey, who's walking who?
-[all laughing]

767
00:30:09,908 --> 00:30:12,212
[Crow] I am a fugitive
from a Gulf station.

768
00:30:12,245 --> 00:30:13,780
[dogs barking]

769
00:30:13,813 --> 00:30:16,215
[Tom] So, the cops
have a dogwalking service.

770
00:30:19,018 --> 00:30:21,220
[Mike] Must escape
from Barbra Woodhouse.

771
00:30:23,990 --> 00:30:25,157
[Crow] Peekaboo.

772
00:30:27,127 --> 00:30:29,495
[Tom] Yeah, it's one of them
horseless carriagers.

773
00:30:30,730 --> 00:30:32,698
[Mike] It was nice of his mom
to pick him up

774
00:30:32,732 --> 00:30:34,467
after prison escape practice.

775
00:30:37,603 --> 00:30:40,473
[Crow] Is this one
of the photographic effects?

776
00:30:44,310 --> 00:30:47,480
[Mike] Dogs are gonna flag down
a Buick and give chase.

777
00:30:47,547 --> 00:30:48,882
[all laughing]

778
00:30:50,082 --> 00:30:52,752
[Tom] Whoa. Ten minutes out
of prison and he scores.

779
00:30:56,990 --> 00:31:00,327
[Crow] Hayley Mills in the
Amazing Transparent Trap.

780
00:31:00,360 --> 00:31:01,360
[Tom] Oh wow.

781
00:31:02,228 --> 00:31:04,097
[Mike]
Not really adjusting to society,

782
00:31:04,130 --> 00:31:05,832
would you take me back
to prison?

783
00:31:05,865 --> 00:31:07,933
[Tom] ♪ Hold on
Putting on a top hat ♪

784
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:11,972
♪ Dusting off my white tie
Breaking out of jail ♪

785
00:31:12,005 --> 00:31:13,873
[music playing]

786
00:31:13,907 --> 00:31:15,308
[Mike] Trip's getting
better and better.

787
00:31:17,043 --> 00:31:19,578
[Crow]
I'm lonely, please love me.

788
00:31:22,148 --> 00:31:24,083
[Tom]
Ah, now you can barely see him.

789
00:31:24,117 --> 00:31:25,183
Camouflocking.

790
00:31:26,152 --> 00:31:28,955
[Mike] Shadowlands 2:
Escape From Oxford.

791
00:31:28,988 --> 00:31:29,988
[Servo laughing]

792
00:31:34,460 --> 00:31:35,695
[Laura] What is it officer?

793
00:31:35,728 --> 00:31:36,895
[Tom]
Is your knob turned to Bob?

794
00:31:36,930 --> 00:31:38,230
A man escaped
from state prison ma'am.

795
00:31:38,263 --> 00:31:39,998
This is just routine.

796
00:31:40,032 --> 00:31:41,867
Who are you
and where have you been?

797
00:31:41,900 --> 00:31:44,570
Oh my name's Matson.
Laura Matson.

798
00:31:44,603 --> 00:31:46,138
This is my husband, John.

799
00:31:46,205 --> 00:31:48,540
We've been to a party
at Pritchards Point.

800
00:31:48,573 --> 00:31:50,710
-I'm afraid he overdid it.
-[Mike] He's a Kennedy.

801
00:31:50,743 --> 00:31:52,745
Uh. May I check
your drivers license, please?

802
00:31:52,778 --> 00:31:53,913
Yes, of course.

803
00:31:54,547 --> 00:31:56,682
[Tom] He's a druggie.

804
00:31:56,715 --> 00:31:58,583
[crickets chirping]

805
00:31:59,918 --> 00:32:02,755
-[Crow] You don't weigh 110..
-What about your husband's?

806
00:32:02,822 --> 00:32:04,890
[Laura] Well that's why,
I'm playing chauffeur. His--

807
00:32:04,923 --> 00:32:08,060
His license was suspended,
uh, drunk driving.

808
00:32:08,093 --> 00:32:09,062
[Tom]
That happened to me, too.

809
00:32:09,095 --> 00:32:10,497
Well, there's no need
to bother him.

810
00:32:10,530 --> 00:32:11,930
He's had enough trouble
for one night

811
00:32:11,998 --> 00:32:13,065
without waking up

812
00:32:13,098 --> 00:32:15,167
and staring into the badge
of an officer.

813
00:32:15,200 --> 00:32:17,070
Very sorry
to have inconvenienced you.

814
00:32:17,137 --> 00:32:18,170
That's quite alright.

815
00:32:18,203 --> 00:32:21,407
[Tom] Just proceed
to next ineffectual policeman.

816
00:32:23,208 --> 00:32:25,378
I think their roadblocks
are too close.

817
00:32:28,782 --> 00:32:32,152
[Tom]
♪ He's amazing and amusing ♪

818
00:32:32,185 --> 00:32:35,722
♪ He's delicious
and nutritious ♪

819
00:32:35,755 --> 00:32:38,457
♪ Two for breakfast, one for ♪

820
00:32:38,490 --> 00:32:40,460
You played it smarter
than I thought you would,

821
00:32:40,493 --> 00:32:43,028
you'd pull that gun,
it would've been all over.

822
00:32:43,062 --> 00:32:45,130
Still don't get it.
Why'd you break me out?

823
00:32:45,163 --> 00:32:46,565
[Mike]
You are Earl, aren't you?

824
00:32:46,598 --> 00:32:48,267
You'll find out when we get
where we're going.

825
00:32:48,333 --> 00:32:51,237
[Crow] She's taking him to
a surprise welcome home party.

826
00:32:51,303 --> 00:32:54,807
-[music playing]
-[Servo humming]

827
00:32:54,873 --> 00:32:55,942
[Tom] You know, North Dakota

828
00:32:55,975 --> 00:32:57,743
film industry never
really took off.

829
00:33:02,082 --> 00:33:06,118
-[Servo humming]
-[Crow] Dorothy returns home.

830
00:33:10,623 --> 00:33:13,058
[Mike] They're on their way
to my grandma's.

831
00:33:13,092 --> 00:33:14,027
I don't need to see this.

832
00:33:15,327 --> 00:33:18,965
[Crow] The sad thing is that
this is their downtown area.

833
00:33:22,368 --> 00:33:24,637
[music playing]

834
00:33:25,938 --> 00:33:27,073
[Tom] Oh,

835
00:33:27,107 --> 00:33:29,208
It's Mary Tyler Moore's
country apartment.

836
00:33:29,275 --> 00:33:30,308
[Crow] Norman?

837
00:33:35,080 --> 00:33:36,282
[Mike] Ah, heh,

838
00:33:37,117 --> 00:33:38,417
uh, my door?

839
00:33:38,450 --> 00:33:40,487
Are you gonna get my...
'cause really,

840
00:33:40,520 --> 00:33:42,588
I mean, we should be g-- well.

841
00:33:44,190 --> 00:33:46,225
[Crow]
Escaped convicts are so rude.

842
00:33:48,962 --> 00:33:50,563
[Tom] Goes without saying,
get off my land.

843
00:33:50,597 --> 00:33:53,098
You come around here
to scare off visitors?

844
00:33:53,132 --> 00:33:54,167
He serves his purpose.

845
00:33:55,167 --> 00:33:58,103
Yeah. I guess everybody does.

846
00:33:58,137 --> 00:34:00,707
Julian, put the car away,
will you? Is Karl waiting?

847
00:34:00,740 --> 00:34:03,075
Been in there chewing up
the carpet for two hours,

848
00:34:03,108 --> 00:34:06,112
-any trouble?
-No. Joey Faust, this is Julian.

849
00:34:06,145 --> 00:34:07,480
[Tom] Cut your hair, hippie.

850
00:34:08,480 --> 00:34:11,183
I've seen him
before in a police bulletin.

851
00:34:11,217 --> 00:34:13,252
[Tom] And on the cover
of the Rolling Stone.

852
00:34:13,285 --> 00:34:14,787
[Crow] Zing.

853
00:34:19,558 --> 00:34:21,993
[Mike]
Well, they have valet parking.

854
00:34:24,163 --> 00:34:27,933
[Tom] So, Julian,
the scene's over.

855
00:34:27,967 --> 00:34:29,235
It's a-- you wanna just...

856
00:34:30,268 --> 00:34:32,272
-cut any time--
-[Mike] It's over.

857
00:34:32,305 --> 00:34:36,242
-[Tom] Go on, go on. Well,
-[Mike] Go-- go away.

858
00:34:36,275 --> 00:34:38,477
[Crow] Ooh, they've got
Protestant Revival furniture.

859
00:34:39,978 --> 00:34:42,115
[Mike]
Hello, so sorry for your loss.

860
00:34:42,182 --> 00:34:43,282
[Laura] Thank you.

861
00:34:45,417 --> 00:34:48,187
[Krenner]
Well, I was beginning to worry.

862
00:34:48,220 --> 00:34:51,123
Major Krenner, may I present,
Joey Faust.

863
00:34:51,190 --> 00:34:53,592
[Tom] Did we overdress
for North Dakota?

864
00:34:53,658 --> 00:34:54,860
Major.

865
00:34:54,893 --> 00:34:56,695
Does it surprise you,
Mr. Faust?

866
00:34:56,728 --> 00:34:57,863
It's for real?

867
00:34:58,697 --> 00:35:01,267
-What army?
-Well, there have been several.

868
00:35:01,300 --> 00:35:02,302
Take your choice.

869
00:35:03,102 --> 00:35:04,303
Please, Mr. Faust.

870
00:35:05,270 --> 00:35:06,138
It's lovely. What is it?

871
00:35:07,307 --> 00:35:09,108
It's only bit of shrapnel,
it's a keepsake.

872
00:35:09,175 --> 00:35:10,208
[Tom] Gimme. Gimme.

873
00:35:10,242 --> 00:35:13,212
That piece of shrapnel ended
the Major's military career.

874
00:35:13,245 --> 00:35:14,413
[Krenner]
That would be all, Laura.

875
00:35:14,447 --> 00:35:16,982
-[Crow] And his sex life.
-[Krenner] Um,

876
00:35:18,383 --> 00:35:20,753
you did well my dear,
now run along and freshen up

877
00:35:20,787 --> 00:35:23,222
while Mr. Faust
and I discuss business.

878
00:35:23,288 --> 00:35:24,657
If you'll excuse me.

879
00:35:24,690 --> 00:35:25,692
Major.

880
00:35:25,725 --> 00:35:27,293
[Tom] Apparently,
I'm not fresh.

881
00:35:27,327 --> 00:35:29,262
Go ahead. Take off the coat
if you wish.

882
00:35:29,295 --> 00:35:30,930
[Krenner] I know
you'd like an explanation.

883
00:35:30,963 --> 00:35:32,063
[Mike] I'm effeminate

884
00:35:32,097 --> 00:35:33,498
because my mother
was overbearing.

885
00:35:35,502 --> 00:35:37,237
Why did you set up the break?

886
00:35:37,870 --> 00:35:38,603
Well, I can use you.

887
00:35:39,738 --> 00:35:42,073
You have got a reputation
in the underworld somewhat

888
00:35:42,107 --> 00:35:44,577
of a genius when it comes
to opening safes and vaults.

889
00:35:44,643 --> 00:35:46,212
[Crow humming]

890
00:35:46,245 --> 00:35:49,015
They must have dug
that shrapnel out of your head.

891
00:35:49,048 --> 00:35:50,550
What kind of an idiot are you?

892
00:35:50,583 --> 00:35:53,018
Krenner, I can't poke my nose
through a bank door

893
00:35:53,085 --> 00:35:54,387
without getting it blown off.

894
00:35:54,420 --> 00:35:56,688
Every newspaper
in the country's got my picture.

895
00:35:56,722 --> 00:35:59,092
You're bitter, Faust.
Mean and bitter.

896
00:35:59,125 --> 00:36:00,960
You trust no one
and you hate everyone.

897
00:36:01,027 --> 00:36:03,863
You're the kind of man
I need and understand.

898
00:36:03,897 --> 00:36:05,297
I know all about
your background.

899
00:36:05,330 --> 00:36:07,433
[Crow] Your marriage
to Lana Turner, your--

900
00:36:07,467 --> 00:36:08,902
What you know about me,

901
00:36:08,935 --> 00:36:10,870
you couldn't have gotten out
of any newspaper.

902
00:36:10,937 --> 00:36:13,472
Well, I check closely
on all the people I use.

903
00:36:13,538 --> 00:36:15,473
I know about the wife
who turned you in.

904
00:36:15,542 --> 00:36:17,108
-[Servo gasping]
-And the child

905
00:36:17,142 --> 00:36:18,710
you've never been
allowed to see.

906
00:36:18,743 --> 00:36:20,713
[Mike] Ouch. That was mean, Tad.

907
00:36:20,747 --> 00:36:22,515
I'm really vulnerable
right now.

908
00:36:23,382 --> 00:36:24,583
[Mike] Oh.

909
00:36:24,617 --> 00:36:27,653
-I may owe you something...
-[Tom] Something?

910
00:36:27,687 --> 00:36:30,590
But if you ever mention
my daughter's name again

911
00:36:30,657 --> 00:36:32,958
you'll have another hole
in your head, I promise you!

912
00:36:33,025 --> 00:36:34,827
[Tom] Susie. Susie.
Susie. Susie.

913
00:36:34,860 --> 00:36:36,995
-Susie. Susie. Susie. Susie.
-If you wish, Mr. Faust.

914
00:36:37,028 --> 00:36:38,597
Susie. Susie. Susie.
Susie. Susie. Susie.

915
00:36:38,630 --> 00:36:40,767
Yes, your background
explains your behavior.

916
00:36:40,800 --> 00:36:41,800
[Tom] Susie. Susie.

917
00:36:41,833 --> 00:36:46,338
[theme music playing]

918
00:36:48,573 --> 00:36:49,442
[Krenner] Now,

919
00:36:50,777 --> 00:36:53,745
we're conducting experiments
requiring fissionable materials.

920
00:36:53,778 --> 00:36:55,480
-[Crow growling]
-[Krenner] Make it real for us.

921
00:36:55,513 --> 00:36:56,682
[Krenner] You'll be well paid.

922
00:36:57,517 --> 00:36:59,285
That's atom bomb stuff.

923
00:36:59,318 --> 00:37:01,953
The government has that locked
up tighter than Fort Knox.

924
00:37:02,053 --> 00:37:02,888
[Krenner] Precisely.

925
00:37:04,457 --> 00:37:06,592
Include me
a long way out, chum.

926
00:37:08,227 --> 00:37:09,628
You're hardly in a position

927
00:37:09,662 --> 00:37:12,298
-to bargain, Mr. Faust.
-[Mike] Bargain, Faust, get it?

928
00:37:12,365 --> 00:37:14,367
-[Crow] Huh? Huh? Uh-huh?
-[Tom] Mm-hmm.

929
00:37:14,400 --> 00:37:16,968
A man with a gun doesn't
have to bargain.

930
00:37:17,002 --> 00:37:18,903
-He gets stuff wholesale.
-[Krenner] True.

931
00:37:18,938 --> 00:37:20,372
[Krenner] I'm certain...

932
00:37:20,405 --> 00:37:22,375
Julian would disagree
with you on that.

933
00:37:22,408 --> 00:37:24,577
[Tom] Yeah, well Julian
can lick-- Oh, hi Julian.

934
00:37:24,610 --> 00:37:26,778
[Faust] What these bullets
will do this time?

935
00:37:26,812 --> 00:37:28,380
It'll rip out your spine

936
00:37:28,413 --> 00:37:30,282
and roll it up like a ball
of string.

937
00:37:31,317 --> 00:37:32,485
What's the score, Krenner?

938
00:37:32,518 --> 00:37:33,818
[Tom]
The Vikings pulled it out.

939
00:37:33,852 --> 00:37:35,420
You'll work for us faithfully,
or you'll be turned over

940
00:37:35,453 --> 00:37:37,823
to the authorities.
I understand there's a reward

941
00:37:37,857 --> 00:37:39,492
of $5000 on your head.

942
00:37:39,558 --> 00:37:40,558
[Servo scoffing]

943
00:37:40,592 --> 00:37:42,027
Just what are these authorities
gonna say

944
00:37:42,060 --> 00:37:43,595
about your part in the break?

945
00:37:43,628 --> 00:37:44,763
[Krenner] The police need know
nothing

946
00:37:44,797 --> 00:37:46,365
of my part in the escape.

947
00:37:46,398 --> 00:37:49,735
You see, they'll also pay me
$5000 for a dead Faust.

948
00:37:49,768 --> 00:37:53,372
[Crow] I must admit that it is
funny, it's not funny ha-ha

949
00:37:53,405 --> 00:37:54,507
but it's droll.

950
00:37:55,507 --> 00:37:57,142
What do you want?

951
00:37:57,175 --> 00:37:58,943
[Krenner]
That'll all be for now, Julian.

952
00:37:58,978 --> 00:38:00,713
[Tom] Go freshen up, Julian.

953
00:38:00,747 --> 00:38:02,448
[Krenner]
Come along to the laboratory.

954
00:38:02,482 --> 00:38:03,648
[Mike]
And hear Alexander's Rag Time--

955
00:38:03,682 --> 00:38:05,183
-You got a lab here?
-Oh yes.

956
00:38:05,217 --> 00:38:07,185
We're completely equipped
for our experiments.

957
00:38:07,218 --> 00:38:09,622
[Crow] We got a grant
from viewers like you.

958
00:38:09,655 --> 00:38:11,023
[Krenner]
I made an extensive search

959
00:38:11,057 --> 00:38:13,392
before selecting this ranch
as an operational base.

960
00:38:13,425 --> 00:38:14,860
[Tom]
That's really interesting.

961
00:38:16,595 --> 00:38:17,997
[water bubbling]

962
00:38:18,030 --> 00:38:21,167
-[Mike] Rhoda, we're coming up
-[Tom] I'm here.

963
00:38:23,468 --> 00:38:25,270
[Crow] Night of the Living
Jerry Stiller.

964
00:38:25,303 --> 00:38:27,473
As you can see, this is quite
a serious business.

965
00:38:27,507 --> 00:38:28,573
Dr. Ulaf.

966
00:38:28,640 --> 00:38:30,242
[Tom]
I'm not looking at my boogers.

967
00:38:30,275 --> 00:38:31,143
[Krenner] Come here.

968
