[all laughing]

1204
00:50:23,488 --> 00:50:25,323
[Tom] Alright,
I better go fall for this.

1205
00:50:29,493 --> 00:50:32,097
Locks may mean nothing
to you, mister,

1206
00:50:32,130 --> 00:50:33,898
but a lock saved my life once.

1207
00:50:37,135 --> 00:50:38,070
Faust?

1208
00:50:38,170 --> 00:50:40,372
[Crow] You rubbing a glass
against the wall?

1209
00:50:40,405 --> 00:50:42,140
You in there, Faust?

1210
00:50:42,173 --> 00:50:43,808
[Tom] I was trying
to read Anna Quindlen.

1211
00:50:45,210 --> 00:50:48,547
[Mike] Mr. Wilson and Dennis
the Menace: The Final Conflict.

1212
00:50:51,182 --> 00:50:53,285
[Crow]
And I get to keep the glass.

1213
00:50:56,622 --> 00:50:59,057
[Tom] We'll turn this
into a bolero jacket.

1214
00:51:04,897 --> 00:51:07,765
Quit drooling
on the carpet. Hey.

1215
00:51:07,798 --> 00:51:08,667
[Servo slurping]

1216
00:51:09,802 --> 00:51:13,438
-[music playing]
-[Servo humming]

1217
00:51:13,505 --> 00:51:16,173
[Crow] I'm breaking all the
guinea pigs out of this place.

1218
00:51:20,478 --> 00:51:23,982
[Tom] I just got to find that
Snap-on Tools calendar.

1219
00:51:25,650 --> 00:51:28,085
[Mike] This place is probably
crawling

1220
00:51:28,120 --> 00:51:29,420
with whiskery old guards.

1221
00:51:30,488 --> 00:51:32,690
[Crow] He's gonna hide
Easter baskets now.

1222
00:51:35,260 --> 00:51:39,330
[Tom] Bud, Princess, Beaver,
Wally, Robbie, Chip, Ernie.

1223
00:51:44,035 --> 00:51:46,337
[Mike] Hey!
This is not my beautiful house.

1224
00:51:47,938 --> 00:51:49,207
Doctor Ulaf.

1225
00:51:49,273 --> 00:51:50,808
[Tom] You know what,
I'm going to rearrange?

1226
00:51:53,778 --> 00:51:56,113
[Crow] That screen is
so the guinea pig can undress.

1227
00:51:58,217 --> 00:52:00,185
[Tom]
This lock has meaning to me.

1228
00:52:01,620 --> 00:52:05,457
[Mike] There's gotta be a glass
I can rub against this door.

1229
00:52:05,490 --> 00:52:07,158
[Crow]
I'm feeling good right now.

1230
00:52:07,192 --> 00:52:09,060
♪ Scooby dooby dooby doo ♪

1231
00:52:12,630 --> 00:52:15,033
[Tom] I just have this craving
for some Sociables.

1232
00:52:19,570 --> 00:52:22,207
[Mike]
Now, I can sew that button on.

1233
00:52:24,008 --> 00:52:26,545
[Crow] Time to slip into
something more comfortable.

1234
00:52:28,613 --> 00:52:31,448
[Tom]
Surprise! Happy birthday to you.

1235
00:52:32,650 --> 00:52:35,853
What are you doing?
What is it you want up here?

1236
00:52:35,887 --> 00:52:38,390
Take it easy, doc.
I just came up to see you.

1237
00:52:38,423 --> 00:52:40,458
Thought maybe,
they had you locked up in there.

1238
00:52:40,525 --> 00:52:43,395
Not I. Only what's left
of my soul.

1239
00:52:43,428 --> 00:52:46,363
-[Tom] I'm kidding, anyway.
-I need to get some answers.

1240
00:52:46,397 --> 00:52:48,132
You seem 'bout the only one
around here

1241
00:52:48,165 --> 00:52:50,268
who isn't a member
of Krenner's fan club.

1242
00:52:51,435 --> 00:52:53,438
I have nothing to say.

1243
00:52:53,472 --> 00:52:54,605
I'm a servant.

1244
00:52:54,638 --> 00:52:56,708
Major Krenner does my thinking
for me.

1245
00:52:56,742 --> 00:53:00,012
[Crow] Hm, his robe
is by Lands of Austria. Wow.

1246
00:53:00,045 --> 00:53:01,613
What's he got on you, doc?

1247
00:53:01,647 --> 00:53:03,248
[Tom] He knows I'm German.

1248
00:53:04,482 --> 00:53:08,152
-Why do you ask me these things?
-So I can get some answers!

1249
00:53:08,185 --> 00:53:09,922
How'd you get mixed up in this?

1250
00:53:10,788 --> 00:53:12,957
My daughter. He's holding her.

1251
00:53:13,758 --> 00:53:15,360
In that cheese box?

1252
00:53:15,360 --> 00:53:17,128
Well, open it up
and let her out.

1253
00:53:17,162 --> 00:53:19,730
Please Mr. Faust, I do not joke.
Not about my daughter.

1254
00:53:19,763 --> 00:53:21,867
[Tom] Although I hear
she swims out to troop ships.

1255
00:53:21,900 --> 00:53:23,302
[Servo laughing]
But that's a joke.

1256
00:53:24,502 --> 00:53:27,305
[Mike] That hurt, if it makes
you feel good to hurt me

1257
00:53:27,338 --> 00:53:29,840
-then so be it.
-Alright, doc.

1258
00:53:29,873 --> 00:53:31,643
Start at the beginning
how'd you get over here?

1259
00:53:31,677 --> 00:53:34,712
[Crow] We carpooled
from the Ottoman Empire.

1260
00:53:34,778 --> 00:53:37,482
At the end of Second World War,
I fled my own country

1261
00:53:37,548 --> 00:53:40,085
-with my baby daughter.
-[Servo humming]

1262
00:53:40,152 --> 00:53:41,452
My wife had died of experiments,

1263
00:53:41,518 --> 00:53:43,153
I had been forced
to perform on her

1264
00:53:43,187 --> 00:53:45,490
-in the concentration camp.
-Your own wife?

1265
00:53:46,725 --> 00:53:49,093
All my patients wore hoods,

1266
00:53:49,127 --> 00:53:50,095
I couldn't see their faces.

1267
00:53:51,630 --> 00:53:54,532
I didn't know my wife was one
of them until it was too late.

1268
00:53:54,565 --> 00:53:57,335
[Mike] I bet this place is loud
in a rainstorm.

1269
00:53:57,402 --> 00:53:59,337
It still don't explain
how you got here.

1270
00:53:59,403 --> 00:54:01,840
-[Crow] I took a damn boat!
-I came to this country

1271
00:54:01,873 --> 00:54:04,608
as a refugee. No one knew
I was a scientist.

1272
00:54:04,642 --> 00:54:07,412
-Except spies like Krenner.
-[Tom] Spies like us.

1273
00:54:07,478 --> 00:54:09,580
After my wife died, I wanted no
more of science,

1274
00:54:09,613 --> 00:54:11,348
-Spies like us!
-I decided to come here.

1275
00:54:11,382 --> 00:54:12,817
-[Servo laughing]
-[Mike] It's not funny.

1276
00:54:12,883 --> 00:54:14,552
-Here's your daughter?
-[Tom] Spies like us!

1277
00:54:14,585 --> 00:54:17,388
If I do not do as I'm told,
she will be killed.

1278
00:54:17,455 --> 00:54:19,723
-[Tom] Spies like us!
-If I carry out the orders,

1279
00:54:19,757 --> 00:54:21,392
-she isn't killed.
-[Servo coughing]

1280
00:54:24,328 --> 00:54:26,463
[Tom] Hey, let's go spy
on the guinea pigs

1281
00:54:26,497 --> 00:54:29,400
-while they take their showers.
-...that you could open it.

1282
00:54:29,433 --> 00:54:31,335
Not now, doc. I got problems.

1283
00:54:32,270 --> 00:54:34,372
Please, Mr. Faust,
release my daughter

1284
00:54:34,405 --> 00:54:36,173
and take her to some safe place.

1285
00:54:36,207 --> 00:54:38,310
Knock it off, doc,
I got my own troubles.

1286
00:54:38,343 --> 00:54:39,410
Then you can't open it.

1287
00:54:41,378 --> 00:54:43,548
I could open
that thing blindfolded.

1288
00:54:43,582 --> 00:54:45,917
[Mike] I'll open it,
but locks mean nothing to me.

1289
00:54:49,587 --> 00:54:53,257
Hmm. Quite the little
psychologist, aren't ya, doc?

1290
00:54:54,625 --> 00:54:56,327
That's good reasoning.

1291
00:54:56,360 --> 00:54:59,497
[Crow] Dear God, its Kitty
Carlisle and she's packing heat!

1292
00:55:00,832 --> 00:55:04,635
[Tom] Oh, congratulations
on your wedding, Miss Havisham.

1293
00:55:04,668 --> 00:55:06,838
[Crow]
Certainly a daring nightgown.

1294
00:55:06,872 --> 00:55:08,840
-[Mike] Rated to 30 below.
-Downstairs, Faust.

1295
00:55:08,907 --> 00:55:11,008
And please try not
to be amusing.

1296
00:55:11,042 --> 00:55:12,810
[Tom] Hey don't worry.
No problem on this end.

1297
00:55:14,112 --> 00:55:15,580
Good night, Doctor Ulaf.

1298
00:55:17,982 --> 00:55:20,152
[Crow]
Say good night to my hamsters.

1299
00:55:22,387 --> 00:55:24,188
[Servo sobbing]

1300
00:55:25,223 --> 00:55:29,127
[Tom] I love this door.
Is this a panel craft?

1301
00:55:29,193 --> 00:55:30,995
[Servo sobbing]

1302
00:55:31,028 --> 00:55:33,598
[Tom]
I love a well-armed woman.

1303
00:55:33,632 --> 00:55:35,833
[Mike] Sit down at that organ
and play Stardust.

1304
00:55:38,235 --> 00:55:40,438
[Crow]
Arnold Schwarzenegger, pregnant.

1305
00:55:42,707 --> 00:55:45,477
[Tom] Oh, he grabs his neck
more than Chuck Heston.

1306
00:55:45,510 --> 00:55:47,545
What's next Mata Hari,
you gonna shoot me?

1307
00:55:49,280 --> 00:55:51,648
Krenner probably will
when he gets back.

1308
00:55:51,682 --> 00:55:53,485
He doesn't
appreciate disloyalty.

1309
00:55:53,485 --> 00:55:56,922
-[Mike] He hates Jack Morris.
-Julian's the loyal type.

1310
00:55:56,988 --> 00:55:57,888
[Tom] And bitter.

1311
00:55:57,922 --> 00:55:59,723
All it got him was a lump
in the head.

1312
00:55:59,790 --> 00:56:02,260
-[Crow] Backgammon?
-He's still out.

1313
00:56:02,293 --> 00:56:03,495
I couldn't bring him around.

1314
00:56:05,230 --> 00:56:07,332
[Tom] See? He's pregnant.
I can't believe it.

1315
00:56:07,365 --> 00:56:09,000
[Laura] What's on your mind?

1316
00:56:09,033 --> 00:56:11,535
For a dame that's supposed
to be so greedy,

1317
00:56:11,568 --> 00:56:13,805
you don't know anything
about playing a hole card.

1318
00:56:13,838 --> 00:56:15,173
Oh?

1319
00:56:15,207 --> 00:56:17,508
Ever think how much that ray
would be worth to a guy

1320
00:56:17,542 --> 00:56:19,910
-who wanted to rob a bank?
-[Mike] With a team of hamsters?

1321
00:56:19,910 --> 00:56:21,747
With that thing, I could get
into every vault

1322
00:56:21,813 --> 00:56:23,080
in the country
in broad daylight.

1323
00:56:24,548 --> 00:56:25,917
Dream on, buster.

1324
00:56:25,950 --> 00:56:27,918
-[Tom] I hate that show.
-Sounds pretty.

1325
00:56:27,985 --> 00:56:30,122
Of course, to take
on jobs like that,

1326
00:56:30,188 --> 00:56:31,588
a fella'd have
to have a little help.

1327
00:56:32,590 --> 00:56:35,093
Now, splitting the take
of a few of those

1328
00:56:35,160 --> 00:56:37,828
would pay you a lot more
than Krenner will ever give you

1329
00:56:37,862 --> 00:56:38,830
in a lifetime.

1330
00:56:38,863 --> 00:56:40,532
[Crow] He gives me
a million dollars a day.

1331
00:56:40,565 --> 00:56:42,600
You need background music
for this commercial.

1332
00:56:44,802 --> 00:56:46,737
Full orchestra and violin.

1333
00:56:46,737 --> 00:56:49,540
-[Mike] Maybe Bobby McFerrin.
-Don't believe me.

1334
00:56:49,573 --> 00:56:50,308
Don't be a gambler.

1335
00:56:51,442 --> 00:56:53,612
Should I gamble on
having my throat slit by Krenner

1336
00:56:53,645 --> 00:56:55,547
or being shot by you?

1337
00:56:55,613 --> 00:56:57,148
-[Crow] Go for the curtain!
-Honey, that's a chance

1338
00:56:57,215 --> 00:56:58,315
you'd have to take,

1339
00:56:58,382 --> 00:57:00,218
Just like the risk
I run every time

1340
00:57:00,252 --> 00:57:00,985
I get under that ray.

1341
00:57:02,187 --> 00:57:04,388
Well, if you don't take
a chance, you'll never know.

1342
00:57:04,422 --> 00:57:05,990
[Crow] That's in The Road
Less Traveled.

1343
00:57:06,023 --> 00:57:07,225
I'll keep it in mind.

1344
00:57:09,193 --> 00:57:12,330
-[Crow] It's under control Hank.
-Thanks Julian.

1345
00:57:12,330 --> 00:57:14,332
He-- he was trying
to make a deal.

1346
00:57:14,365 --> 00:57:16,367
You must have heard
what he said.

1347
00:57:16,400 --> 00:57:18,168
He was talking a double-cross.

1348
00:57:18,937 --> 00:57:20,672
Yeah, I heard him.

1349
00:57:20,705 --> 00:57:23,073
Sounded like you were listening
awful close, too.

1350
00:57:23,107 --> 00:57:24,308
[Tom]
She's got Ernest Borgnine arms.

1351
00:57:24,342 --> 00:57:25,610
Keep quiet about this, Julian.

1352
00:57:25,643 --> 00:57:27,878
-Keep quiet and I'll help you.
-[Crow] With your math.

1353
00:57:27,912 --> 00:57:29,580
You know that Krenner's gonna
blame you

1354
00:57:29,613 --> 00:57:30,882
for letting him
out of his room.

1355
00:57:30,915 --> 00:57:32,617
We've got to help
each other now.

1356
00:57:32,650 --> 00:57:34,218
-[door knocking]
-[Krenner] Julian.

1357
00:57:34,252 --> 00:57:36,020
-[Tom] Lucy, I'm home!
-[Krenner] Julian.

1358
00:57:36,053 --> 00:57:37,588
[Julian]
Let's get him in the room.

1359
00:57:37,622 --> 00:57:39,323
-[door knocking]
-[Krenner] Julian!

1360
00:57:39,390 --> 00:57:40,725
[Tom] Action Protection league,
let us in!

1361
00:57:40,758 --> 00:57:41,758
-[door knocking]
-[Julian] I'm coming.

1362
00:57:41,792 --> 00:57:43,027
-Julian! Open this door.
-[Julian] I'm coming.

1363
00:57:43,093 --> 00:57:44,862
[Crow] Give someone else
a crack of this house.

1364
00:57:47,265 --> 00:57:48,567
What happened?

1365
00:57:48,600 --> 00:57:49,933
-Where were you?
-[Mike] We had a date.

1366
00:57:49,967 --> 00:57:51,102
Getting the broom.

1367
00:57:51,135 --> 00:57:53,337
Wanted to sweep
up that bottle I dropped.

1368
00:57:53,370 --> 00:57:56,340
I told you not to take
your eyes off of Faust's door.

1369
00:57:56,407 --> 00:57:58,543
Oh, him. He's out cold.

1370
00:57:58,610 --> 00:58:00,845
I went in, picked up the bottle,
figured you didn't want him

1371
00:58:00,878 --> 00:58:02,780
nipping in the morning
when he wakes up.

1372
00:58:02,813 --> 00:58:04,048
[Tom] Want some griddle cakes?

1373
00:58:04,048 --> 00:58:05,583
[Krenner] You did right.

1374
00:58:05,617 --> 00:58:07,585
He's got to be sober.
Good night.

1375
00:58:07,618 --> 00:58:09,587
-Night.
-[Mike] Night, Herr Goebbels.

1376
00:58:10,688 --> 00:58:13,592
Yeah, I broke one
of the gophers earlier, sorry.

1377
00:58:13,625 --> 00:58:15,760
[Tom] The organ plays
a pivotal part in this movie,

1378
00:58:15,793 --> 00:58:16,593
doesn't it?

1379
00:58:19,063 --> 00:58:22,333
-[Crow] Why'd you gob on me?
-What you do that for?

1380
00:58:22,367 --> 00:58:23,568
Save your own skin?

1381
00:58:23,635 --> 00:58:25,168
[Crow] My skin is beyond saving.

1382
00:58:25,237 --> 00:58:27,672
This idea
of yours does interest me.

1383
00:58:27,705 --> 00:58:29,840
I figure you need me
more than I need you.

1384
00:58:29,873 --> 00:58:31,608
[Tom] Baby, I'm-a need you.

1385
00:58:34,812 --> 00:58:38,750
[Mike humming
"Addams Family" theme]

1386
00:58:38,783 --> 00:58:40,652
[Crow] Good morning.

1387
00:58:40,718 --> 00:58:43,153
[Tom] Yeah. Breakfast
with Dorothy Parker.

1388
00:58:43,187 --> 00:58:45,757
[Mike] Gotta get out
and cut hay but first, a gimlet.

1389
00:58:47,292 --> 00:58:50,060
[Tom] Seems to be a kind
of a dark side to this house.

1390
00:58:51,695 --> 00:58:53,163
[Crow]
I can't stand the tension,

1391
00:58:53,230 --> 00:58:54,732
somebody play that organ!

1392
00:58:55,633 --> 00:58:57,302
[Mike]
Where's that olive I was saving?

1393
00:58:59,470 --> 00:59:00,905
I thought you were up
in the lab.

1394
00:59:02,373 --> 00:59:03,675
[Tom] I was.

1395
00:59:05,008 --> 00:59:06,843
-What was that for?
-Stop trying.

1396
00:59:06,912 --> 00:59:09,747
Don't even think about it.
Julian told me about your talk

1397
00:59:09,780 --> 00:59:10,948
-with Faust.
-[Crow] Don't even think

1398
00:59:10,982 --> 00:59:12,817
about asking me
if I was up in the lab.

1399
00:59:12,883 --> 00:59:14,685
-Didn't make any deal.
-No, not yet.

1400
00:59:14,718 --> 00:59:16,787
But I know you.
My memory is too good.

1401
00:59:16,820 --> 00:59:17,755
I remember our other deals.

1402
00:59:17,788 --> 00:59:19,357
You don't think I
gave you enough

1403
00:59:19,390 --> 00:59:20,492
of a share, do you?

1404
00:59:21,125 --> 00:59:22,960
What are you talking about?

1405
00:59:23,027 --> 00:59:25,697
What could I possibly have
in common with Faust?

1406
00:59:25,730 --> 00:59:28,232
-He's a hood.
-Just the type you go for.

1407
00:59:28,265 --> 00:59:30,302
Laura, I don't care
what you do with your life

1408
00:59:30,368 --> 00:59:33,437
but when it interferes
with my plans, I draw the line.

1409
00:59:33,470 --> 00:59:34,738
[Mike]
I want you to go to college.

1410
00:59:38,643 --> 00:59:39,743
[Crow]
You were about to ask me

1411
00:59:39,777 --> 00:59:41,745
if I was up in the lab again,
weren't you?

1412
00:59:41,778 --> 00:59:44,115
Lay off the vodka. I want
you ready when I need you.

1413
00:59:46,883 --> 00:59:49,853
[Tom]
♪ As long as he needs me. ♪

1414
00:59:52,523 --> 00:59:54,258
[Tom]
Well, how about some coffee?

1415
00:59:54,292 --> 00:59:57,195
If you will relax, Mr. Faust,
there will be no pain.

1416
00:59:57,228 --> 00:59:59,263
You may lose consciousness
for a few moments

1417
00:59:59,297 --> 01:00:01,032
after you become invisible, but

1418
01:00:01,065 --> 01:00:02,900
there should
be no other ill effect.

1419
01:00:02,967 --> 01:00:04,635
How do you know
it'll work on a human?

1420
01:00:04,668 --> 01:00:05,770
Do you know what'll happen?

1421
01:00:05,803 --> 01:00:07,838
We made hundreds
of such experiments,

1422
01:00:07,872 --> 01:00:09,240
there can be no slip-up.

1423
01:00:09,307 --> 01:00:10,775
[Tom]
Unless something goes wrong.

1424
01:00:15,480 --> 01:00:16,880
Come on, doctor, we don't
have all night.

1425
01:00:16,913 --> 01:00:18,783
[Crow] David McCallum was booked
for midnight.

1426
01:00:20,418 --> 01:00:22,153
[Mike] We were just in the lab,
weren't we?

1427
01:00:22,220 --> 01:00:23,688
-[Servo mimics slap]
-[Mike] Aah!

1428
01:00:25,288 --> 01:00:26,992
[Mike] Is that the toner up
there for the--

1429
01:00:27,025 --> 01:00:28,793
when they make people
visible again?

1430
01:00:28,827 --> 01:00:31,495
[Crow humming]

1431
01:00:31,562 --> 01:00:32,963
[Crow] Nothing to worry about.

1432
01:00:32,997 --> 01:00:34,565
[Tom] I'm in kind of a rush
today,

1433
01:00:34,598 --> 01:00:35,867
so, I pushed fewer buttons.

1434
01:00:35,933 --> 01:00:37,235
[Mike] Ah, gentle pressure.

1435
01:00:38,035 --> 01:00:39,370
[Crow] Oh, he's a thick man.

1436
01:00:40,572 --> 01:00:42,307
[Tom] I don't like peering up
his nostrils, though.

1437
01:00:44,575 --> 01:00:47,745
[Tom] Isn't someone gonna
swab me with antiseptic?

1438
01:00:47,778 --> 01:00:48,947
[Mike] Please?

1439
01:00:51,848 --> 01:00:54,085
[Crow] It's just going to make
a little hamster-size

1440
01:00:54,118 --> 01:00:55,887
invisible hole
in the middle of 'em.

1441
01:00:58,055 --> 01:01:00,257
[Tom] Look at that one, Henry.
It's the biggest guinea pig

1442
01:01:00,290 --> 01:01:01,258
I ever saw.

1443
01:01:01,292 --> 01:01:03,393
[Mike] Accept the pain, man,
I've sucked up

1444
01:01:03,427 --> 01:01:05,930
a lot more radiation
than you ever will, chunky boy.

1445
01:01:11,602 --> 01:01:14,072
[Crow] The visible six banger.
You build it.

1446
01:01:18,142 --> 01:01:20,177
[Servo growling]

1447
01:01:20,243 --> 01:01:21,745
[Mike] My pancreas!

1448
01:01:23,882 --> 01:01:26,117
[Crow] He disappeared
with an audible ping.

1449
01:01:26,150 --> 01:01:27,285
He's going to Notions!

1450
01:01:29,587 --> 01:01:34,192
[theme music playing]

1451
01:01:39,930 --> 01:01:41,898
[Mike]
Wow. He leaves quite an odor.

1452
01:01:43,400 --> 01:01:45,403
What's happened? Is he alright?

1453
01:01:45,470 --> 01:01:46,870
[Crow] Doc,
that's not his wrist.

1454
01:01:46,903 --> 01:01:49,440
His pulse is quite rapid,
but that is to be expected.

1455
01:01:49,473 --> 01:01:52,910
-Why is he breathing like that?
-It may be shock.

1456
01:01:52,943 --> 01:01:55,447
Faust, can you hear me?
Can you speak?

1457
01:01:55,480 --> 01:01:56,913
Faust, try to sit up.

1458
01:01:56,947 --> 01:01:58,883
[Tom]
Talking to the wrong end.

1459
01:01:58,917 --> 01:02:01,885
He stopped breathing.
Well, do something!

1460
01:02:02,920 --> 01:02:04,188
[Crow] Don't do that!

1461
01:02:05,388 --> 01:02:06,757
-He's gone.
-What?

1462
01:02:09,127 --> 01:02:10,360
[gasping]

1463
01:02:13,730 --> 01:02:16,000
-[Crow] He gave me a wet Willie.
-It's Faust. He's here.

1464
01:02:16,067 --> 01:02:17,802
Faust, answer me, you--
[grunting]

1465
01:02:17,868 --> 01:02:19,903
[Crow] I found him.
[Crow laughing]

1466
01:02:19,937 --> 01:02:22,773
Faust, listen to me,
you'll remain invisible

1467
01:02:22,840 --> 01:02:24,542
until I want to turn it off,
so you--

1468
01:02:25,743 --> 01:02:28,078
[Crow]
There's a guinea pig on my neck!

1469
01:02:28,112 --> 01:02:30,482
[Mike] That's some form
of pure energy, Captain.

1470
01:02:30,548 --> 01:02:32,250
[Faust]
If I choke you hard enough,

1471
01:02:32,283 --> 01:02:33,650
you'll bring me back.

1472
01:02:33,717 --> 01:02:34,785
[Tom] Boy, the worst
a guinea pig

1473
01:02:34,818 --> 01:02:36,220
ever did
was piddle on him.

1474
01:02:39,090 --> 01:02:41,992
[Faust] I'm just showing you
how badly you need me

1475
01:02:42,025 --> 01:02:45,463
-and my loyalty costs money.
-[Crow] Oh, sorry Ollie.

1476
01:02:45,497 --> 01:02:47,365
I told you you'd be well paid.

1477
01:02:47,398 --> 01:02:50,167
[Faust] You neglected one thing,
chum, how much?

1478
01:02:50,233 --> 01:02:52,068
-A thousand dollars.
-[Faust laughing]

1479
01:02:52,102 --> 01:02:53,537
A thousand every time you do
a job.

1480
01:02:53,570 --> 01:02:57,408
[Faust] Krenner, if I'm gonna
be hit, I want money.

1481
01:02:57,442 --> 01:02:58,608
Lots of money.

1482
01:02:59,443 --> 01:03:00,878
What do you expect?
I can't pay--

1483
01:03:00,945 --> 01:03:04,215
[Faust] Oh yes, you can, Major.
I'm sure you can.

1484
01:03:04,248 --> 01:03:06,050
[Tom] Oh, you're gonna
pay me, Norton.

1485
01:03:06,117 --> 01:03:08,987
[Faust] We can talk it
over in private. Downstairs?

1486
01:03:09,020 --> 01:03:11,822
[Mike] Good idea. The hamsters
are trying to sleep up here.

1487
01:03:11,888 --> 01:03:13,892
[Faust] Over here Major.
I'm waiting.

1488
01:03:13,925 --> 01:03:16,127
[Crow] As it's implied that
I'm over here.

1489
01:03:18,128 --> 01:03:19,730
After you, Major.

1490
01:03:19,763 --> 01:03:21,332
[Tom] I'm so invisible right now

1491
01:03:21,332 --> 01:03:23,033
I've never been
less not invisible.

1492
01:03:27,405 --> 01:03:29,007
[laughing]

1493
01:03:29,040 --> 01:03:30,440
[Mike] Damn you all to hell.

1494
01:03:31,808 --> 01:03:32,977
Anyway.

1495
01:03:33,810 --> 01:03:37,215
So you don't think a thousand
is enough, hmm?

1496
01:03:37,248 --> 01:03:39,050
[Crow] Hang on, I'm snagged
on the banister.

1497
01:03:39,117 --> 01:03:41,085
[Faust]
You're a real case, Major.

1498
01:03:41,118 --> 01:03:43,487
You think everyone's supposed
to jump when you open

1499
01:03:43,520 --> 01:03:46,323
your big, fat mouth. Well,
you've forgotten one thing.

1500
01:03:46,357 --> 01:03:48,225
-[Mike] Your purse.
-Forgotten what?

1501
01:03:48,258 --> 01:03:50,060
[Faust] You gotta have me.

1502
01:03:50,093 --> 01:03:51,595
[Tom]
♪ You gotta have heart. ♪

1503
01:03:51,628 --> 01:03:53,597
[Faust] Now whatever you're
up to, I'm worth plenty to you,

1504
01:03:53,630 --> 01:03:54,598
or you wouldn't have gone to

1505
01:03:54,632 --> 01:03:56,833
all that trouble of
breaking me out?

1506
01:03:56,867 --> 01:03:59,237
You're a dead duck without me.

1507
01:03:59,270 --> 01:04:03,140
-[Crow] Am I on speakerphone?
-What do you have in mind?

1508
01:04:03,173 --> 01:04:07,043
[Faust] Well, walking
into that nuclear bomb's worth

1509
01:04:07,110 --> 01:04:08,612
about 25 grand.

1510
01:04:10,147 --> 01:04:11,615
[Tom] Oh, Abbott, I--

1511
01:04:12,417 --> 01:04:13,550
[Faust]
Get a little air in here.

1512
01:04:14,685 --> 01:04:17,322
Twenty five grand, you're--
you're mad! I don't have

1513
01:04:17,355 --> 01:04:19,923
-that kind of money.
-Then you do the job, Major.

1514
01:04:19,957 --> 01:04:22,427
[Tom] I'm scratching
my butt right now, you know.

1515
01:04:22,460 --> 01:04:25,863
[Faust] That's the figure,
25 grand in small bills,

1516
01:04:25,897 --> 01:04:28,032
C.O.D., take it or leave it.

1517
01:04:28,065 --> 01:04:30,500
-[Crow] Your fly's open.
-All right.

1518
01:04:30,568 --> 01:04:33,137
You'll go tonight.
Laura will drive you.

1519
01:04:33,170 --> 01:04:34,872
[Tom]
What, you made transparent men

1520
01:04:34,938 --> 01:04:36,373
by the millions, Pittsburgh.

1521
01:04:37,775 --> 01:04:40,310
[Mike] Oppenheimer cupped
Einstein's breasts

1522
01:04:40,343 --> 01:04:43,847
as Niels Bohr let his smock slip
to the lab floor.

1523
01:04:43,880 --> 01:04:44,882
Oh, this is hot.

1524
01:04:49,653 --> 01:04:50,855
[Crow] Jeannie.

1525
01:04:53,657 --> 01:04:55,525
[Tom] I don't get science.

1526
01:04:56,493 --> 01:04:57,662
[Mike] Is that Brut?

1527
01:04:59,097 --> 01:05:01,165
[Crow] Well I suppose
they do those things.

1528
01:05:03,167 --> 01:05:04,235
[Tom]
Hey, when you're transparent,

1529
01:05:04,268 --> 01:05:06,037
mess with a fuse box.
It's funny.

1530
01:05:07,003 --> 01:05:08,705
[Mike] You can't put
a hot tub in here,

1531
01:05:08,738 --> 01:05:10,307
you need at least 100 amps.

1532
01:05:12,175 --> 01:05:16,447
[Crow] That's not really worth
standing up for, but I'm up.

1533
01:05:16,480 --> 01:05:19,883
[Tom] Wheel Of Fortune!

1534
01:05:21,085 --> 01:05:22,853
[Mike] Why does he have
a napkin dispenser?

1535
01:05:22,887 --> 01:05:23,653
[Tom] I don't--

1536
01:05:24,322 --> 01:05:26,657
Great. Here comes Chaz the Spaz.

1537
01:05:29,460 --> 01:05:33,030
No, I will not trade lunches
with you, Chaz.

1538
01:05:35,767 --> 01:05:37,602
[Mike]
My cigarette lacks flavor.

1539
01:05:37,635 --> 01:05:38,970
What's wrong with you?

1540
01:05:39,003 --> 01:05:41,505
I don't know. Why, I just
thought I heard something.

1541
01:05:41,538 --> 01:05:44,142
[Crow] Aye, matey. Hard to port!

1542
01:05:44,175 --> 01:05:45,542
[Crow humming]

1543
01:05:45,575 --> 01:05:47,845
Back in ten minutes.
I gotta check the other post.

1544
01:05:47,878 --> 01:05:50,313
[Mike] Yeah, right, light
a match, will ya?

1545
01:05:50,347 --> 01:05:51,448
[music playing]

1546
01:05:52,650 --> 01:05:54,818
[Tom] Don't-- Jeez,
they should lock that thing.

1547
01:06:00,023 --> 01:06:02,225
[Crow] He's moshing by himself.

1548
01:06:02,258 --> 01:06:03,560
[Mike] This happens every day

1549
01:06:03,593 --> 01:06:05,028
whether there's an invisible man
or not.

1550
01:06:09,300 --> 01:06:10,633
[Crow mimics dog yelping]

1551
01:06:12,670 --> 01:06:15,740
[Mike] No, he's stealing
their walleye minnows.

1552
01:06:15,773 --> 01:06:16,940
[Tom]
Or maybe he should've brought

1553
01:06:16,973 --> 01:06:18,575
a transparent tote bag.

1554
01:06:21,478 --> 01:06:22,680
[Crow] Be sure to sign out.

1555
01:06:24,248 --> 01:06:25,883
[Mike] Is he gonna plant bulbs?

1556
01:06:27,083 --> 01:06:28,585
[Crow] Here,
they're just flexing

1557
01:06:28,618 --> 01:06:30,187
their special effects muscles.

1558
01:06:32,557 --> 01:06:34,392
[Tom]
Gerry Cooney, security guard.

1559
01:06:39,030 --> 01:06:43,067
[Tom singing] ♪ Put a hundred
down and buy a car ♪

1560
01:06:43,100 --> 01:06:44,968
You didn't see anyone
during all this?

1561
01:06:45,002 --> 01:06:47,303
Well, no sir. I--
I heard a clicking noise

1562
01:06:47,337 --> 01:06:49,073
like the wheel of the vault
was turning, and I--

1563
01:06:49,107 --> 01:06:51,508
I told him about it but neither
one of us saw anything.

1564
01:06:51,575 --> 01:06:53,577
That's right, there was nothing
when I started to check

1565
01:06:53,610 --> 01:06:54,612
the other guard post.

1566
01:06:54,645 --> 01:06:56,047
[Crow]
I don't live with my mother.

1567
01:06:56,080 --> 01:06:57,280
Then what?

1568
01:06:57,347 --> 01:07:00,483
Well, I-- I turned around and a,

1569
01:07:00,550 --> 01:07:03,187
and the vault door was open,
all by itself and--

1570
01:07:03,220 --> 01:07:05,555
and I got up to check it
and-- and I pulled my gun.

1571
01:07:05,588 --> 01:07:07,992
And something was all over me
and then the next thing I know,

1572
01:07:08,025 --> 01:07:09,993
-I'm-- I'm waking up.
-[Tom] Uh-huh.

1573
01:07:10,027 --> 01:07:11,928
What about the alarm panel,
when did it get wrecked?

1574
01:07:11,962 --> 01:07:15,298
I don't know, Mr. Drake,
it was okay when I came on duty.

1575
01:07:15,332 --> 01:07:17,502
It couldn't have happened while
I was checking posts

1576
01:07:17,535 --> 01:07:19,803
or he'd have noticed anyone
tampering with it.

1577
01:07:19,837 --> 01:07:21,605
It's set up with each of us
covering the other.

1578
01:07:21,638 --> 01:07:23,807
Yes, but it's quite obvious,
neither one of you were covering

1579
01:07:23,840 --> 01:07:25,475
the other. In fact,
there was no coverage at all.

1580
01:07:25,508 --> 01:07:27,177
[Tom] Can we
finish being fired?

1581
01:07:28,945 --> 01:07:30,815
[Mike]
There's a little stormtrooper

1582
01:07:30,848 --> 01:07:33,417
who lives in my pants.
He whispers things to me.

1583
01:07:33,450 --> 01:07:35,352
Alright, doctor, what was it
that you wanted to tell me

1584
01:07:35,385 --> 01:07:36,553
about the guinea pig?

1585
01:07:36,587 --> 01:07:39,990
-It was only a matter of time.
-[Crow] I've grown to love him.

1586
01:07:40,023 --> 01:07:42,693
It died despite
of resistance it was developing

1587
01:07:42,727 --> 01:07:44,895
-against radiation,
-[Tom] She lived a good life.

1588
01:07:44,928 --> 01:07:46,230
What about Faust?

1589
01:07:46,263 --> 01:07:47,465
It's too early to tell

1590
01:07:47,498 --> 01:07:49,600
but each time
it will take longer to reduce

1591
01:07:49,633 --> 01:07:53,803
him to invisibility, less to
return him to normal states.

1592
01:07:53,837 --> 01:07:56,373
He will also develop
a resistance.

1593
01:07:56,407 --> 01:07:58,275
What about the new material,
the X-13?

1594
01:07:58,308 --> 01:08:01,412
I need more time to study it.
Its properties are different

1595
01:08:01,445 --> 01:08:05,215
from other nuclear materials.
I do not like keeping it here.

1596
01:08:05,248 --> 01:08:07,585
It's bombardment ratio
is also much lower,

1597
01:08:07,618 --> 01:08:10,253
-[Mike] It's vomit ratio?
-[Tom] Vova-- Vobamat.

1598
01:08:12,155 --> 01:08:13,790
-Strawberry jam.
-Uh,

1599
01:08:14,558 --> 01:08:17,293
-afraid of an explosion?
-Yes.

1600
01:08:17,327 --> 01:08:20,397
[Tom] Unt I'm afraid of strudel,
though, I don't know why.

1601
01:08:20,430 --> 01:08:23,267
-It's --
-Well use the X-13 on Faust.

1602
01:08:23,300 --> 01:08:25,368
It could mean his death.

1603
01:08:25,402 --> 01:08:28,405
Doctor, I'm not concerned with
the welfare of one man.

1604
01:08:28,438 --> 01:08:30,875
I must know the full potential
of your invention

1605
01:08:30,942 --> 01:08:33,643
because my aim is to make
an entire army invisible,

1606
01:08:33,677 --> 01:08:35,278
do you understand?
An entire army.

1607
01:08:35,345 --> 01:08:36,947
I did not agree to kill a man

1608
01:08:36,980 --> 01:08:38,982
by deliberate
radiation poisoning.

1609
01:08:40,017 --> 01:08:42,285
You're too old-fashioned
to be a genius.

1610
01:08:43,787 --> 01:08:45,655
[doors closing]

1611
01:08:57,033 --> 01:09:01,772
-Say Mike. What do we have here?
-Yeah, it looks really cool.

1612
01:09:01,805 --> 01:09:02,807
Whatever it is.

1613
01:09:02,873 --> 01:09:05,108
Oh, come on, it's my
Magichord 2000

1614
01:09:05,142 --> 01:09:08,012
Lowrey electronic organ.
Pretty neat, huh?

1615
01:09:08,078 --> 01:09:10,013
-I'll say.
-What'd it do?

1616
01:09:10,080 --> 01:09:12,048
Oh well, give
a listen. Here.

1617
01:09:12,082 --> 01:09:13,683
[organ playing]

1618
01:09:15,718 --> 01:09:18,222
This is neat.
This will be fun forever.

1619
01:09:18,255 --> 01:09:21,025
-We'll never get sick of this.
-No, uh.

1620
01:09:26,530 --> 01:09:30,333
Say Tom, I've got,
three rubber bands in my room.

1621
01:09:30,367 --> 01:09:32,603
-I'm there man.
-Huh, huh? Okay, let's go.

1622
01:09:33,970 --> 01:09:37,340
[organ playing]

1623
01:09:37,373 --> 01:09:38,708
Cambot. Lights please.

1624
01:09:39,910 --> 01:09:41,378
Ah, perfect.

1625
01:09:46,450 --> 01:09:48,485
Perhaps the most volatile
and feisty

1626
01:09:48,518 --> 01:09:51,888
of Henry the VIII's six wives
was Anne Boleyn.

1627
01:09:51,922 --> 01:09:55,825
[organ playing]

1628
01:10:00,998 --> 01:10:02,365
What are you-- Hi.

1629
01:10:03,633 --> 01:10:07,037
Just, uh, putting on, uh,
doing some uh,

1630
01:10:07,103 --> 01:10:09,138
Lights please, Cambot.

1631
01:10:09,172 --> 01:10:12,342
I was just-- let me get--
Latin rhythm.

1632
01:10:12,375 --> 01:10:14,645
-Oh.
-We'll be right back.

1633
01:10:14,678 --> 01:10:16,713
-Yeah, I was just uh--
-Yeah, sure.

1634
01:10:16,747 --> 01:10:18,082
-Something I heard.
-We know.

1635
01:10:18,115 --> 01:10:19,015
Nothing we haven't done.

1636
01:10:19,048 --> 01:10:24,422
[organ playing]

1637
01:10:26,757 --> 01:10:30,793
-[Crow laughing]
-[Mike] Rick quick, I was--

1638
01:10:30,827 --> 01:10:32,462
Something every
cheap safecracker

1639
01:10:32,528 --> 01:10:34,765
in the country's been
dreaming of.

1640
01:10:34,798 --> 01:10:36,767
Too bad the doc didn't invent
it years ago

1641
01:10:36,800 --> 01:10:39,437
for the right people.
Wouldn't be cooped up here now.

1642
01:10:39,503 --> 01:10:41,372
[Crow] Give me five, homey.

1643
01:10:41,405 --> 01:10:42,873
You better lay off
the giggle water.

1644
01:10:42,907 --> 01:10:44,675
He can't use you drunk,
you know.

1645
01:10:44,708 --> 01:10:47,678
It's a case of my using him,
now come on with a drink.

1646
01:10:47,678 --> 01:10:49,680
[Tom] What am I,
invisible here?

1647
01:10:49,713 --> 01:10:51,782
I could have walked
into that vault

1648
01:10:51,815 --> 01:10:54,485
in broad daylight,
had done the same thing.

1649
01:10:54,518 --> 01:10:56,787
So easy, I felt like
I was taking Krenner's

1650
01:10:56,820 --> 01:11:00,057
-money away from him.
-[Crow] Hi pop!

1651
01:11:00,090 --> 01:11:02,827
This time you do
it the hard way.

1652
01:11:02,860 --> 01:11:04,462
-In daylight.
-[Mike] On stilts.

1653
01:11:04,528 --> 01:11:06,430
Well, through with me
so soon, huh?

1654
01:11:06,497 --> 01:11:08,398
-Gonna get me killed off.
-Oh, no not at all.

1655
01:11:08,432 --> 01:11:10,533
But I have learned that security
has been tripled

1656
01:11:10,567 --> 01:11:11,868
on the
vaults during the night shift.

1657
01:11:11,902 --> 01:11:14,972
Six men. But it hasn't been
strengthened during the day.

1658
01:11:15,038 --> 01:11:17,340
Since where do you get
these gems?

1659
01:11:17,407 --> 01:11:19,410
He has sources,
you better believe it.

1660
01:11:19,443 --> 01:11:20,677
One more haul,
and we'll have enough

1661
01:11:20,710 --> 01:11:22,980
for our present supply
of work. Uh-uh.

1662
01:11:23,013 --> 01:11:24,782
-[Tom] Not the Glenfiddich.
-You won't need this,

1663
01:11:24,815 --> 01:11:27,483
-we're ready in the lab.
-[Crow] Thanks a lot, Bill W.

1664
01:11:34,558 --> 01:11:37,828
[Mike] Did I do something wrong
to make him not slap me?

1665
01:11:39,763 --> 01:11:41,732
My girdle is killing me.

1666
01:11:46,570 --> 01:11:48,238
[Crow] Mr. Keisterman's mailbox.

1667
01:11:51,442 --> 01:11:53,810
[Mike] You see, doctor,
my mom loved my sister

1668
01:11:53,843 --> 01:11:55,612
a lot more than me and...

1669
01:11:55,645 --> 01:11:59,617
[Tom] Tonight on 21, Charles Van
Doren vs. Leopold Stokowski.

1670
01:12:01,618 --> 01:12:04,153
[Crow] Go ahead
on invisible booth five.

1671
01:12:06,123 --> 01:12:07,323
Hurry up.

1672
01:12:07,357 --> 01:12:09,192
[Mike] You've been riding
my tuchis for too long.

1673
01:12:10,927 --> 01:12:13,063
[Tom] Oliver Reed's
looking better than usual.

1674
01:12:15,665 --> 01:12:16,767
I cannot.

1675
01:12:16,833 --> 01:12:18,502
[Crow] That bratwurst
is repeating on me.

1676
01:12:19,737 --> 01:12:22,472
[Mike] I guess,
I could've done that.

1677
01:12:22,505 --> 01:12:23,573
[Tom] It didn't seem too hard.

1678
01:12:25,975 --> 01:12:27,510
Great. A guinea pig got me.

1679
01:12:29,178 --> 01:12:32,682
[Mike] I'll make any one
invisible for $49.95.

1680
01:12:34,150 --> 01:12:36,587
[Crow] Well, either he's
invisible or the rice is done.

1681
01:12:41,092 --> 01:12:44,227
[Tom] Forestry, changing the
face of northern Michigan.

1682
01:12:46,530 --> 01:12:48,865
[Mike] Jackie Kennedy,
after the tragedy.

1683
01:12:48,898 --> 01:12:50,600
She just took off
in the convertible.

1684
01:12:52,302 --> 01:12:53,603
[Tom] Took a little cruise.

1685
01:12:58,608 --> 01:13:01,412
You can't get away with
this in broad daylight, Joey.

1686
01:13:01,445 --> 01:13:03,647
Why don't you stop running
Kranner's errands?

1687
01:13:03,713 --> 01:13:06,517
-[Crow] I'm naked you know.
-Lets not get any more involved.

1688
01:13:06,550 --> 01:13:08,418
[Faust]
Don't get emotional baby,

1689
01:13:08,485 --> 01:13:10,887
you're nice as a playmate.
Let's keep it that way.

1690
01:13:10,920 --> 01:13:12,757
Let's understand that, hmm?

1691
01:13:12,790 --> 01:13:13,957
They may not see you,

1692
01:13:13,990 --> 01:13:15,558
but they're
bound to see the lead container

1693
01:13:15,625 --> 01:13:16,960
when you try to get it out.

1694
01:13:17,027 --> 01:13:18,695
[Faust] Got it all wrong, honey.

1695
01:13:18,728 --> 01:13:20,697
Today's the day
we take the bank.

1696
01:13:20,730 --> 01:13:23,000
In broad daylight? You're crazy!

1697
01:13:23,033 --> 01:13:25,135
[Faust] You're talking
to a professional, remember?

1698
01:13:25,168 --> 01:13:26,603
I don't have to worry
about time locks

1699
01:13:26,637 --> 01:13:28,238
or anything like that.

1700
01:13:28,272 --> 01:13:31,575
Just walk in, pack up
the loose cash, and walk out

1701
01:13:31,608 --> 01:13:33,577
and you, sweetheart,
are gonna be waiting for me.

1702
01:13:33,610 --> 01:13:35,578
[Crow] That guy just lights up
the screen.

1703
01:13:35,612 --> 01:13:37,347
Oh, don't even say it.

1704
01:13:37,380 --> 01:13:39,382
You'll cut'll be forty percent.

1705
01:13:42,352 --> 01:13:45,255
[Tom] Chief, there's an
invisible man to see you.

1706
01:13:45,288 --> 01:13:46,690
[Faust] I'll have to climb out.

1707
01:13:47,490 --> 01:13:49,092
[Crow] See? I climbed out.

1708
01:13:50,893 --> 01:13:53,830
[Mike] I'll have to walk
into the bank.

1709
01:13:53,863 --> 01:13:56,267
And now the lighter side
of banking.

1710
01:14:01,838 --> 01:14:05,008
[Crow] Oh, that door
is on the fritz, I'll fix that.

1711
01:14:05,042 --> 01:14:08,578
[Tom] Goodness, the steaks
and chops are calling me.

1712
01:14:08,612 --> 01:14:11,682
[Mike] Anyone would notice
if I got a nice big porterhouse

1713
01:14:11,715 --> 01:14:12,850
and brought it back in here.

1714
01:14:15,718 --> 01:14:16,787
[Crow] Rod Serling.

1715
01:14:16,853 --> 01:14:19,455
[Tom] A deposit, submitted
for your approval.

1716
01:14:21,692 --> 01:14:23,693
[Mike] Guess that line's
for women only.

1717
01:14:25,462 --> 01:14:26,630
Eliza Doolittle.

1718
01:14:37,007 --> 01:14:40,177
[Tom] Well, "Beginner's Guide
to Bank Security".

1719
01:14:41,512 --> 01:14:42,745
[Mike] Joey's gonna steal

1720
01:14:42,778 --> 01:14:44,715
everybody's real
estate abstract.

1721
01:14:44,715 --> 01:14:45,748
[Tom] No.

1722
01:14:45,748 --> 01:14:47,550
[Crow]
That's a tiny ping pong table.

1723
01:14:51,588 --> 01:14:54,290
[Tom] Hey, Kevin Costner
as Jim Garrison.

1724
01:14:54,357 --> 01:14:55,758
Wait.

1725
01:14:57,862 --> 01:15:00,063
[Mike]
She's very Olivia da Havilland.

1726
01:15:02,967 --> 01:15:04,433
[Tom] All this happened
in Point Break

1727
01:15:04,500 --> 01:15:05,768
only you could see everyone.

1728
01:15:05,802 --> 01:15:08,738
-[Mike] Unfortunately,
-[Crow] Oh, great.

1729
01:15:08,772 --> 01:15:10,573
I'm just transparent-ish.

1730
01:15:13,343 --> 01:15:16,045
-Oh my Lord.
-[Tom] It's Donald Nixon.

1731
01:15:17,113 --> 01:15:18,482
Don't anybody move,
you won't get hurt.

1732
01:15:19,683 --> 01:15:22,052
[Crow] He'd be less conspicuous
without the gun and the money.

1733
01:15:27,023 --> 01:15:28,825
What's the matter?
What changed?

1734
01:15:28,858 --> 01:15:30,260
Drive. Drive. Drive!

1735
01:15:30,327 --> 01:15:32,428
[Mike] What's the matter?
I oughta--

1736
01:15:35,298 --> 01:15:38,535
[Tom] I just know I'm gonna show
up in News of the Weird now.

1737
01:15:38,568 --> 01:15:41,137
[Mike] No blinker. They're
just exacerbating their crime.

1738
01:15:45,242 --> 01:15:47,677
Thank you very much
for your cooperation, Miss.

1739
01:15:47,710 --> 01:15:49,713
-I only hope it does some good.
-I'm sure it will.

1740
01:15:49,747 --> 01:15:51,213
-[phone ringing]
-[Tom] He was invisible

1741
01:15:51,248 --> 01:15:53,817
-but I'm positive he was black.
-Security.

1742
01:15:53,850 --> 01:15:55,218
Good day now.

1743
01:15:55,252 --> 01:15:57,988
[Tom] He's been downgraded
to a partially-visible man.

1744
01:15:58,022 --> 01:15:58,988
Okay.

1745
01:15:59,823 --> 01:16:01,325
Guess we can't stop it now.

1746
01:16:02,192 --> 01:16:03,893
[Tom]
Someone ordered pizza for us.

1747
01:16:03,927 --> 01:16:06,763
Story's out. A reporter
was at the police station

1748
01:16:06,797 --> 01:16:07,663
when the alarm came in.

1749
01:16:08,732 --> 01:16:10,700
Well, there's no doubt
it was Faust.

1750
01:16:10,733 --> 01:16:12,970
Everyone
at the bank's identified him.

1751
01:16:13,037 --> 01:16:13,737
Well, what can we do?

1752
01:16:15,038 --> 01:16:18,475
The man makes himself invisible.
Locks mean nothing to him.

1753
01:16:18,508 --> 01:16:21,077
-[Tom] He's smarter than us.
-He did take the X-13.

1754
01:16:21,110 --> 01:16:23,313
What defense do
we have against him?

1755
01:16:23,380 --> 01:16:25,782
Nothing. None.

1756
01:16:25,815 --> 01:16:27,750
[Tom] Maybe cops on bicycles
is the answer.

1757
01:16:29,585 --> 01:16:31,755
[Mike]
We return now to Paris, Texas.

1758
01:16:36,125 --> 01:16:37,260
Hold it here.

1759
01:16:37,293 --> 01:16:39,730
[Crow] I love the smell of
atrazine in the morning.

1760
01:16:41,765 --> 01:16:43,933
[Tom] Oh, looks like he made
the trip to bountiful.

1761
01:16:47,203 --> 01:16:48,938
[hissing]

1762
01:16:51,273 --> 01:16:53,477
[Tom] I'm gonna build a casino,
right here.

1763
01:16:55,178 --> 01:16:57,413
There must be something
we can do.

1764
01:16:57,447 --> 01:16:59,650
Now, if I can just
get down to Ulaf.

1765
01:16:59,717 --> 01:17:02,185
I can't get back to the house
till I'm invisible again.

1766
01:17:02,218 --> 01:17:03,787
[radio jockey] Police,
report the so-called

1767
01:17:03,820 --> 01:17:06,155
invisible man who robbed
the Coatesville national bank

1768
01:17:06,188 --> 01:17:08,325
has been identified
as Joey Faust,

1769
01:17:08,358 --> 01:17:10,127
escaped convict
and safe cracker.

1770
01:17:10,160 --> 01:17:13,363
Witnesses said the robber became
visible before their eyes.

1771
01:17:13,430 --> 01:17:14,530
Citizens are warn--

1772
01:17:14,563 --> 01:17:15,898
[Crow] I wanted to listen
to the Twins.

1773
01:17:15,932 --> 01:17:18,035
He's gotta come back,
and that tramp, too.

1774
01:17:19,402 --> 01:17:21,070
I'll teach them
to double-cross me.

1775
01:17:21,103 --> 01:17:23,040
Take my bags outside and watch
for them.

1776
01:17:23,040 --> 01:17:25,375
-Right.
-[Mike] My own personal MacLeod.

1777
01:17:28,378 --> 01:17:30,580
[Tom] This gives me a chance
to call Peggy, quick.

1778
01:17:30,613 --> 01:17:32,248
I know some people in Mexico.

1779
01:17:32,315 --> 01:17:34,283
We can go there
and start all over again.

1780
01:17:34,317 --> 01:17:36,387
[Tom] Mexico, huh?
Do you know Jose.

1781
01:17:36,420 --> 01:17:38,522
I know what I've been,
Joey, but--

1782
01:17:38,555 --> 01:17:40,790
-I can change all that.
-[Crow] I can be taller.

1783
01:17:40,857 --> 01:17:43,527
-More full-figured.
-What are you doing?

1784
01:17:43,560 --> 01:17:46,530
Giving you your split.
You're on your own, baby.

1785
01:17:46,563 --> 01:17:49,365
But-- but you said
we'd be together.

1786
01:17:51,502 --> 01:17:55,072
Honey, right now, I need a car
more than I need you.

1787
01:17:55,138 --> 01:17:57,273
So you can start walking
any time.

1788
01:17:57,340 --> 01:17:59,575
But you can't just go off
and leave me.

1789
01:18:01,210 --> 01:18:03,647
[Mike]
I'll be right back after this.

1790
01:18:03,713 --> 01:18:04,680
Joey!

1791
01:18:04,715 --> 01:18:07,117
[Tom] He overdid
the Clearasil again.

1792
01:18:07,150 --> 01:18:09,720
-[Faust] Goodbye, Laura.
-[Crow] I tell you, I love you.

1793
01:18:11,655 --> 01:18:13,723
[Crow] Huh. Is this goodbye?

1794
01:18:15,525 --> 01:18:17,827
[Tom] It's a fine
how-do-you-do.

1795
01:18:17,860 --> 01:18:21,263
Oh, well, at least he left me
this pound of ground beef.

1796
01:18:21,297 --> 01:18:22,832
Some patties, I suppose.

1797
01:18:23,900 --> 01:18:25,802
[Mike] Oh, I gotta get
the soybeans in.

1798
01:18:28,172 --> 01:18:30,440
We'll leave everything except
the nuclear material.

1799
01:18:30,507 --> 01:18:31,575
[Crow] Nuke-u-lar!

1800
01:18:31,608 --> 01:18:33,777
The police catch Faust
and hear his story,

1801
01:18:33,810 --> 01:18:34,912
they'll be all over us.

1802
01:18:34,945 --> 01:18:37,013
[Crow] Oh, my Zeitgeist
is gestalting

1803
01:18:37,080 --> 01:18:38,882
and now I have middle Schmitz.

1804
01:18:38,915 --> 01:18:40,017
I will not.

1805
01:18:40,983 --> 01:18:43,420
-What?
-[Tom] Uh, okay I will.

1806
01:18:43,453 --> 01:18:45,988
I have been running away most
of my life.

1807
01:18:46,022 --> 01:18:48,525
[Crow] Now get out of my
dingy furnished laboratory.

1808
01:18:49,860 --> 01:18:50,960
Doctor,

1809
01:18:51,762 --> 01:18:53,363
there's something wrong
with your formula.

1810
01:18:53,397 --> 01:18:55,432
But, we'll work it out
in a new location.

1811
01:18:55,465 --> 01:18:57,533
An invisible army
is worth billions.

1812
01:18:58,402 --> 01:19:00,537
An army of dead men, Major.

1813
01:19:01,505 --> 01:19:04,240
[Mike] Oh, thanks,
Otto Von Bringmedown.

1814
01:19:04,307 --> 01:19:04,942
[laughing]

1815
01:19:06,308 --> 01:19:08,010
I'm taking Marie with me,
I'm sure you'll come along.

1816
01:19:08,043 --> 01:19:08,945
No!

1817
01:19:08,945 --> 01:19:10,047
[Crow]
She didn't finish her supper

1818
01:19:10,080 --> 01:19:11,848
-she should stay in her room.
-Marie, my dear.

1819
01:19:13,850 --> 01:19:15,252
Prison is fun.

1820
01:19:17,620 --> 01:19:19,055
[Mike] What the-- huh?

1821
01:19:19,088 --> 01:19:20,690
[Krenner] Faust! Let me out
of here!

1822
01:19:22,692 --> 01:19:24,627
[Crow] It's super Donald Nixon.

1823
01:19:24,660 --> 01:19:28,132
Doc, what's wrong? Why do I keep
appearing and disappearing?

1824
01:19:28,165 --> 01:19:30,567
I don't know.
I needed more time to study

1825
01:19:30,600 --> 01:19:32,968
the X-13 before trying
it out on you,

1826
01:19:33,002 --> 01:19:33,970
Oh, fine.

1827
01:19:34,037 --> 01:19:35,738
Well, you're gonna do something
about it.

1828
01:19:35,772 --> 01:19:37,907
I've got to be normal,
you're gonna take care of it.

1829
01:19:37,940 --> 01:19:39,910
I will, but not in this place.

1830
01:19:39,943 --> 01:19:41,545
Oh, why not?
I got him locked up.

1831
01:19:41,578 --> 01:19:43,012
I want to be taken away
from here,

1832
01:19:43,045 --> 01:19:45,215
my daughter and I, only then,
I will treat you.

1833
01:19:45,248 --> 01:19:48,117
-But--
-[Tom] You with th-- then the--

1834
01:19:48,185 --> 01:19:49,685
Alright, lets blow out of here.

1835
01:19:49,718 --> 01:19:51,320
[Crow] Lets put our lips
together and blow.

1836
01:19:55,158 --> 01:19:57,093
[Tom]
So why is she dressed for mass?

1837
01:19:57,160 --> 01:20:00,230
[Mike] Man, Houdini couldn't get
her out of that dress.

1838
01:20:00,230 --> 01:20:03,032
-[Julian] Forget something?
-[Crow] Uh, girdles.

1839
01:20:03,098 --> 01:20:06,670
[theme music playing]

1840
01:20:09,238 --> 01:20:10,673
Put that thing down.

1841
01:20:10,707 --> 01:20:13,477
I take my orders from the Major.
I have to.

1842
01:20:13,510 --> 01:20:15,212
-The maj--
-Let me.

1843
01:20:15,245 --> 01:20:16,547
Julian,

1844
01:20:16,613 --> 01:20:17,913
You believe what he told you

1845
01:20:17,947 --> 01:20:20,117
about your son being alive
in prison and in Europe?

1846
01:20:20,183 --> 01:20:22,485
-Yes I do.
-You're a fool.

1847
01:20:23,387 --> 01:20:25,155
Your son's dead, Julian.

1848
01:20:26,022 --> 01:20:27,857
Krenner's been using
you all along.

1849
01:20:27,923 --> 01:20:30,127
[Tom]
But he is in Europe, right?

1850
01:20:30,160 --> 01:20:33,128
[Mike] I don't even have a son.
I'm so stupid.

1851
01:20:34,697 --> 01:20:36,800
[Crow] I've guarded this organ
for five years.

1852
01:20:36,867 --> 01:20:38,268
Suddenly I'm back story.

1853
01:20:41,103 --> 01:20:41,972
She's right.

1854
01:20:43,273 --> 01:20:45,508
So you had to
come back. Come on.

1855
01:20:45,542 --> 01:20:47,743
[Mike] I hate to see
a grown codger cry.

1856
01:20:47,810 --> 01:20:50,080
Told you to hit the road,
it was for your own good.

1857
01:20:50,113 --> 01:20:52,315
But, Joey please,
please give me a chance.

1858
01:20:52,382 --> 01:20:54,850
Sweetheart, you had
your chance. In.

1859
01:20:54,885 --> 01:20:58,120
[Crow] This guy loves locks.
Let's admit it.

1860
01:20:58,153 --> 01:21:00,423
All right, come on.
Come on, Julian.

1861
01:21:00,490 --> 01:21:02,125
[Crow] We are all going
to grandma's

1862
01:21:02,158 --> 01:21:03,927
and we're all going
to have a good time!

1863
01:21:05,928 --> 01:21:07,397
[Tom] Julian. See?

1864
01:21:07,463 --> 01:21:10,733
Do you know Krenner is after
a process so an invisible army

1865
01:21:10,767 --> 01:21:12,435
-can invade your country .
-[Mike] Get off me.

1866
01:21:12,468 --> 01:21:14,337
I don't care about Krenner
or his army, I care about me.

1867
01:21:14,370 --> 01:21:16,038
Then all of this is futile.

1868
01:21:16,072 --> 01:21:18,475
Krenner has found me before.
He'll find me again.

1869
01:21:18,508 --> 01:21:20,510
I've got money. I know places
across the border

1870
01:21:20,577 --> 01:21:22,378
he never heard of.
How long are these treatments

1871
01:21:22,412 --> 01:21:24,247
-gonna take?
-You may someday be declared

1872
01:21:24,280 --> 01:21:27,117
a martyr, Mr. Faust.
A man who sacrificed himself

1873
01:21:27,150 --> 01:21:29,352
so that an invisible army
might not overrun your country!

1874
01:21:29,385 --> 01:21:31,888
What are you trying to pull,
you wanted out of here,

1875
01:21:31,922 --> 01:21:34,357
I made a deal with you, now
you cross me and I'll kill you.

1876
01:21:34,390 --> 01:21:37,493
I'm a dying man, Mr. Faust.
Radiation poisoning.

1877
01:21:37,527 --> 01:21:39,895
-[Mike] Oh, boo-hoo.
-You're dying, too.

1878
01:21:39,930 --> 01:21:42,698
-You're worse off than I am.
-You're lying.

1879
01:21:42,732 --> 01:21:45,035
You have only weeks,
perhaps days, to live.

1880
01:21:45,102 --> 01:21:47,003
[Crow]
Damn you, Uncle Charlie!

1881
01:21:47,037 --> 01:21:48,237
You used me to get away
from Krenner.

1882
01:21:48,272 --> 01:21:50,273
To free Maria.
I had no choice.

1883
01:21:50,307 --> 01:21:52,275
[Mike]
That's one big cow flop.

1884
01:21:52,342 --> 01:21:53,777
C'mon.

1885
01:21:53,810 --> 01:21:56,078
Are you so intent
upon evading the police

1886
01:21:56,145 --> 01:21:57,647
for a few remaining days?

1887
01:21:57,713 --> 01:21:59,115
Don't you care
what Krenner is doing

1888
01:21:59,148 --> 01:22:01,350
-to your country.
-Why should I care?

1889
01:22:01,383 --> 01:22:04,020
What did my country ever do
for me but try and bury me

1890
01:22:04,087 --> 01:22:05,688
in a concrete tomb for the rest
of my life!

1891
01:22:05,722 --> 01:22:07,290
[Crow] Oh, your breath
is not invisible.

1892
01:22:07,323 --> 01:22:08,892
I think only of my child.

1893
01:22:08,925 --> 01:22:10,260
You should think of yours.

1894
01:22:11,962 --> 01:22:13,095
Perhaps you deserve prison.

1895
01:22:14,130 --> 01:22:16,700
But did Maria deserve
what has happened to her?

1896
01:22:16,733 --> 01:22:19,835
Did her mother need to die?
Is this the kind of world

1897
01:22:19,868 --> 01:22:23,038
you want for your child?
That is what an invisible army

1898
01:22:23,072 --> 01:22:24,573
would bring. I've seen it!

1899
01:22:24,607 --> 01:22:26,442
[Crow speaking gibberish]

1900
01:22:30,113 --> 01:22:31,313
How much longer I got?

1901
01:22:32,215 --> 01:22:33,617
A month.

1902
01:22:33,683 --> 01:22:34,883
No more.

1903
01:22:34,917 --> 01:22:37,220
[Tom] So, no sense
in starting a Michener novel.

1904
01:22:39,122 --> 01:22:41,223
[Mike] Here. I sneezed
into that.

1905
01:22:47,397 --> 01:22:49,098
[Crow] It's Big League Chew.

1906
01:22:53,202 --> 01:22:54,203
Don't wait for me.

1907
01:22:55,238 --> 01:22:57,340
[Tom] I'm gonna do me
a little possum hunting.

1908
01:22:57,373 --> 01:22:58,942
Then I'll meet up with you.

1909
01:22:58,975 --> 01:23:01,812
[Mike] This amazing
transparent man stands to win--

1910
01:23:01,845 --> 01:23:03,447
[Crow] Where have you been?

1911
01:23:03,480 --> 01:23:05,782
There's a man who has unlocked
every door

1912
01:23:05,848 --> 01:23:07,450
except the one to his own soul.

1913
01:23:07,483 --> 01:23:08,485
[Tom] Bob Dornan.

1914
01:23:08,518 --> 01:23:10,087
Now he has the key.

1915
01:23:10,120 --> 01:23:12,122
Come, my dear, we must find
a telephone

1916
01:23:12,155 --> 01:23:14,157
-and notify the police.
-[Tom] Daddy,

1917
01:23:14,223 --> 01:23:15,792
could I have a line
at some point?

1918
01:23:18,227 --> 01:23:19,328
[gunfire]

1919
01:23:19,395 --> 01:23:20,797
[Crow] It's open.

1920
01:23:22,698 --> 01:23:23,500
[gunshot]

1921
01:23:23,567 --> 01:23:24,433
[Mike] There.

1922
01:23:27,403 --> 01:23:28,572
[Laura] Joey!

1923
01:23:28,605 --> 01:23:31,107
-[door knocking]
-[Laura] Joey!

1924
01:23:31,140 --> 01:23:32,742
[Tom]
This marathon bar is chewy.

1925
01:23:32,775 --> 01:23:33,577
[Laura] Joey!

1926
01:23:34,177 --> 01:23:35,645
[Laura] Joey!

1927
01:23:37,513 --> 01:23:39,282
Joey will you please,
listen to me--

1928
01:23:39,315 --> 01:23:40,317
Wait for me outside.

1929
01:23:41,818 --> 01:23:42,918
[gunshots]

1930
01:23:42,952 --> 01:23:45,855
[Mike] Now see, I told you
to wait for me outside.

1931
01:23:50,827 --> 01:23:53,397
[Crow] I'll prop your head
up so you can watch Sullivan.

1932
01:23:56,132 --> 01:23:58,300
[Tom] Okay, you guard
the downstairs now.

1933
01:24:00,103 --> 01:24:04,740
[Mike] The irony of the organ
being there is unbelievable.

1934
01:24:04,773 --> 01:24:07,310
[Tom] You shot the only woman
who ever vaguely loved me.

1935
01:24:08,678 --> 01:24:10,713
[Crow]
This is gonna be so great.

1936
01:24:14,283 --> 01:24:15,918
[Mike] You're it. No tag-backs.

1937
01:24:17,387 --> 01:24:19,888
[Tom] Oh, no. It was a bottle
of Jolt Cola.

1938
01:24:22,392 --> 01:24:25,362
[Crow] With each blow he
looks more like James Garner.

1939
01:24:25,395 --> 01:24:27,297
[Mike] Okay, now would be a
good time

1940
01:24:27,330 --> 01:24:28,832
for me to turn transparent.

1941
01:24:30,367 --> 01:24:33,202
[Tom] I'll scrape your scapula
down 'til there's nothing left.

1942
01:24:38,508 --> 01:24:42,712
-You crushed my cookies.
-[Crow] I'm sorry our hygienist

1943
01:24:42,745 --> 01:24:44,213
called in sick, again.

1944
01:24:45,315 --> 01:24:47,650
[Tom] Just takes a minute
so if you just lay there,

1945
01:24:47,683 --> 01:24:50,420
I'll be killing you shortly.
Just hang on.

1946
01:24:50,487 --> 01:24:52,422
[Mike] You want a pop or
anything, I'm in the fridge?

1947
01:24:56,458 --> 01:24:59,995
[Crow] Thought I was done,
but just a few more minutes.

1948
01:25:00,028 --> 01:25:01,730
[Tom] Just got
to get the instructions.

1949
01:25:01,763 --> 01:25:03,232
Do you remember
how this thing works?

1950
01:25:09,638 --> 01:25:11,040
[Crow]
You brute, you brute, you brute,

1951
01:25:11,073 --> 01:25:12,908
you brute, you brute.
[Crow crying]

1952
01:25:14,910 --> 01:25:17,313
Leave my
fissionable material alone.

1953
01:25:18,313 --> 01:25:20,283
[Tom] God, a transparent
logie.

1954
01:25:21,683 --> 01:25:23,987
[Crow] Jack and Jill
went up the hill

1955
01:25:24,020 --> 01:25:26,522
to fetch a pail of heavy water.

1956
01:25:30,927 --> 01:25:32,295
[Mike] In an unrelated event,

1957
01:25:33,897 --> 01:25:36,798
[Tom] All my ex's died in Texas.

1958
01:25:36,867 --> 01:25:39,502
[Crow] Maybe they blew up
ZZ Top. Maybe.

1959
01:25:42,105 --> 01:25:43,405
[Tom] Whoop. Zip.

1960
01:25:44,473 --> 01:25:47,577
It were pretty how my wife
turned all purple. [chuckles]

1961
01:25:50,145 --> 01:25:52,448
[Crow] Sorry folks, it's all
nuclear behind here.

1962
01:25:53,750 --> 01:25:56,352
[Mike]
Are you happy, Mr. Teller?

1963
01:25:56,385 --> 01:25:58,588
-Drake and Smith. Security.
-[Tom] Excuse me.

1964
01:25:58,622 --> 01:26:01,290
Why aren't you folks drinking?
This is Texas.

1965
01:26:01,323 --> 01:26:04,927
Sorry, I got my orders.
No one enters this area.

1966
01:26:04,960 --> 01:26:06,562
Too much fallout for safety.

1967
01:26:07,363 --> 01:26:09,432
Alright officer.
Just a minute.

1968
01:26:09,465 --> 01:26:11,433
[Crow] Yeah I think a couple
of jumbo Slim Jims

1969
01:26:11,467 --> 01:26:13,068
will change your mind.

1970
01:26:13,670 --> 01:26:15,137
They're individually wrapped.

1971
01:26:20,108 --> 01:26:22,145
[Tom] And the
Bookmobile is there.

1972
01:26:24,180 --> 01:26:25,982
[Crow] The binoculars switched
to another viewpoint

1973
01:26:28,183 --> 01:26:29,952
They keep switching.

1974
01:26:29,985 --> 01:26:31,888
[Tom]
Here, these'll blow your mind.

1975
01:26:33,388 --> 01:26:34,823
[Mike]
It's a Frank Gehry building.

1976
01:26:37,060 --> 01:26:39,728
[Crow] Fine. If I can't get near
the fallout, I'll just smoke.

1977
01:26:43,833 --> 01:26:45,702
[Tom] Forget it, Jake.
It's Chinatown.

1978
01:26:47,570 --> 01:26:48,772
Be with
you in a minute Smitty.

1979
01:26:48,805 --> 01:26:49,672
[Mike] Gentle pressure.

1980
01:26:49,705 --> 01:26:51,373
See, did you notice the gentle
pressure?

1981
01:26:51,407 --> 01:26:53,208
Thank you. I learned.

1982
01:26:55,412 --> 01:26:57,747
Well, doctor,
you and your friends

1983
01:26:57,780 --> 01:26:59,582
have succeeded in blowing up
half the county.

1984
01:26:59,615 --> 01:27:00,215
[Crow] Yeah, oops.

1985
01:27:01,350 --> 01:27:03,152
There isn't enough left
out there to make ashes

1986
01:27:04,553 --> 01:27:06,155
I'm deeply sorry, of course.

1987
01:27:06,188 --> 01:27:08,490
-[Tom] Can I go now?
-But as I told you before,

1988
01:27:08,523 --> 01:27:10,293
I didn't do anything by choice.

1989
01:27:10,327 --> 01:27:11,493
[Crow] Yeah.

1990
01:27:11,527 --> 01:27:14,097
I warned Major Krenner of the
danger involved,

1991
01:27:14,130 --> 01:27:16,933
but you must realize,
his was a deranged mind.

1992
01:27:17,000 --> 01:27:18,467
[Crow] Mm-hmm.

1993
01:27:18,500 --> 01:27:22,405
All he could think of was the
creating of an invisible army.

1994
01:27:22,438 --> 01:27:24,640
And the power such a
force would give him.

1995
01:27:24,673 --> 01:27:26,275
-[Tom] Geez, I had to ask.
-You know, doctor,

1996
01:27:26,342 --> 01:27:28,778
this idea of an invisible army
is quite interesting.

1997
01:27:28,812 --> 01:27:30,112
[Crow] But I have to go.

1998
01:27:30,145 --> 01:27:31,547
Imagine what our
counterintelligence could

1999
01:27:31,580 --> 01:27:34,317
accomplish if they were able to
become invisible

2000
01:27:34,350 --> 01:27:35,752
whenever necessary.

2001
01:27:35,785 --> 01:27:37,520
The sector
intelligence agency

2002
01:27:37,553 --> 01:27:39,555
has already discussed
the possibilities...

2003
01:27:39,622 --> 01:27:41,257
[Tom] So don't try to
lord over me.

2004
01:27:41,290 --> 01:27:44,327
But my friend,
think of the danger

2005
01:27:44,393 --> 01:27:46,128
if the secret were
stolen from us.

2006
01:27:46,928 --> 01:27:48,463
It has happened before,
you know.

2007
01:27:48,497 --> 01:27:50,065
[Tom]
You big sack of Kraut gas.

2008
01:27:50,098 --> 01:27:51,200
What was that you were saying?

2009
01:27:51,233 --> 01:27:52,435
Perhaps it would be better if we

2010
01:27:52,468 --> 01:27:55,003
let the secret die with
Major Krenner and Joey Faust.

2011
01:27:56,338 --> 01:27:58,140
It's a serious problem.

2012
01:28:01,810 --> 01:28:03,412
What would you do?

2013
01:28:03,478 --> 01:28:05,180
[Mike]
What-- what was the question?

2014
01:28:05,247 --> 01:28:06,115
[Tom] Was that for us?

2015
01:28:06,983 --> 01:28:09,618
[Mike] What would we do?
I don't know.

2016
01:28:09,652 --> 01:28:11,620
[Crow] I didn't know there would
be a test.

2017
01:28:11,653 --> 01:28:14,257
[Mike] Something to think about,
though, hmm.

2018
01:28:15,525 --> 01:28:17,393
[doors closing]

2019
01:28:27,170 --> 01:28:29,205
Wow. What an interesting
dilemma.

2020
01:28:29,238 --> 01:28:30,807
Crow, what would you do?

2021
01:28:30,873 --> 01:28:33,608
-What would you do?
-Oh, that's easy.

2022
01:28:33,677 --> 01:28:35,645
If I were past mid-field,
I'd go for it.

2023
01:28:35,678 --> 01:28:36,778
Maybe just punt.

2024
01:28:36,845 --> 01:28:39,182
Oh, that's not what they mean.
Oh you...

2025
01:28:39,248 --> 01:28:41,250
-incorrigible little robot.
-[laughs]

2026
01:28:41,283 --> 01:28:44,087
No, the question is...
if you had a choice between

2027
01:28:44,120 --> 01:28:46,022
having your own invisible army

2028
01:28:46,022 --> 01:28:49,092
or blowing up a county in Texas,
which would it be?

2029
01:28:49,158 --> 01:28:50,560
Oh, Mike.
Come on, that's easy.

2030
01:28:50,593 --> 01:28:53,162
Under any circumstances,
blowing up a county in Texas

2031
01:28:53,195 --> 01:28:54,463
is justifiable.

2032
01:28:54,530 --> 01:28:56,532
[laughs]

2033
01:28:57,767 --> 01:28:59,568
-What-- what?
-Tom...

2034
01:28:59,602 --> 01:29:01,770
-What?
-My family's from Texas.

2035
01:29:01,803 --> 01:29:04,307
Oh, God. Oh, Mike.
No-- no, I had no idea.

2036
01:29:04,340 --> 01:29:07,277
I was just trying-- no--
Oh, God. Mike, I'm so sorry.

2037
01:29:07,310 --> 01:29:09,378
I'm kidding.
They're from Wisconsin.

2038
01:29:09,445 --> 01:29:11,080
I don't care what you
do to Texas.

2039
01:29:12,282 --> 01:29:14,117
I'm joking. Anyway...

2040
01:29:14,183 --> 01:29:15,618
We've got a letter to read.

2041
01:29:15,652 --> 01:29:18,053
Uh, it's actually
a transparent letter.

2042
01:29:20,022 --> 01:29:22,425
Put that up on still store
there, Cambot.

2043
01:29:23,525 --> 01:29:25,027
It's from Kathleen Donovan

2044
01:29:25,060 --> 01:29:27,630
and she writes, "Here's a little
invention for you."

2045
01:29:27,697 --> 01:29:29,932
And there's a nice little
picture there you can see.

2046
01:29:29,965 --> 01:29:31,233
Let's see. Yeah.

2047
01:29:31,300 --> 01:29:33,970
"Fill Tom Servo's head with
a creamy custardy yogurt

2048
01:29:34,003 --> 01:29:37,005
and you get... voila!
Soft-servo."

2049
01:29:37,073 --> 01:29:38,273
What do you know?

2050
01:29:38,340 --> 01:29:39,808
That's from Kathleen Donovan.

2051
01:29:39,842 --> 01:29:41,143
[Tom]
What happened to the letter?

2052
01:29:41,210 --> 01:29:43,578
-It's gone. It's transparent.
-[Tom] Oh, I see.

2053
01:29:43,612 --> 01:29:45,682
-[laughing]
-Thanks, Kathleen.

2054
01:29:45,715 --> 01:29:48,583
Mike, I have an invisible army,
you know.

2055
01:29:48,617 --> 01:29:49,952
They're right behind me.

2056
01:29:49,985 --> 01:29:51,287
No, no, no you don't.

2057
01:29:51,353 --> 01:29:52,688
My invisible army
is right behind me

2058
01:29:52,755 --> 01:29:54,723
and they just whomped
on your invisible army.

2059
01:29:54,757 --> 01:29:55,658
-Did not!
-Did too!

2060
01:29:55,725 --> 01:29:56,858
-Did not!
-Did too!

2061
01:29:56,925 --> 01:29:58,795
Crow T. Robot and Tom Servo...

2062
01:29:58,828 --> 01:30:01,197
what a couple of wacky,
mixed up bots.

2063
01:30:01,230 --> 01:30:02,998
-I'll see you later.
-Okay.

2064
01:30:05,502 --> 01:30:09,305
Hey. Look at you, you are a
skinny young man.

2065
01:30:09,338 --> 01:30:11,807
Here, have another
one of my rock hard,

2066
01:30:11,840 --> 01:30:13,608
flavorless butter cookies.

2067
01:30:23,953 --> 01:30:26,255
You know,
it's a wonderful dilemma.

2068
01:30:26,322 --> 01:30:28,792
Do I put an ice auger
through his chest

2069
01:30:28,825 --> 01:30:31,593
or do I enjoy another
delicious piece

2070
01:30:31,593 --> 01:30:33,662
of his wonderful
apple pandowdy?

2071
01:30:34,463 --> 01:30:36,865
Either way, I win!
[chuckles]

2072
01:30:38,567 --> 01:30:39,935
Well, Auntie McFrank,

2073
01:30:40,002 --> 01:30:41,737
it looks like you're over
your disappointment.

2074
01:30:48,777 --> 01:30:50,613
[clattering]

2075
01:30:51,213 --> 01:30:57,620
Squanto!

2076
01:30:59,688 --> 01:31:01,723
A Warriors Tale.

2077
01:31:01,757 --> 01:31:03,592
[slow theme music playing]

2078
01:32:03,518 --> 01:32:07,423
You see? I touch it,
it does it no harm.

